WEBVTT

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.044
Welcome to our Education Support Staff
Professional Learning

00:00:08.044 --> 00:00:08.473
Workshop.

00:00:08.473 --> 00:00:11.240
Today we're going to explore Autistic
experience

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:14.961
and what it means for access, belonging
and partnership across

00:00:14.961 --> 00:00:15.801
the school day.

00:00:15.801 --> 00:00:19.255
Keep the students you work with at the
centre of the day.

00:00:19.255 --> 00:00:22.652
We'll build our understanding, look
closely at everyday school

00:00:22.652 --> 00:00:23.148
routines

00:00:23.148 --> 00:00:26.697
and consider what we can change in the
environment, the task and

00:00:26.697 --> 00:00:28.147
the support we offer.

00:00:28.147 --> 00:00:30.743
Our starting point is curiosity.

00:00:31.105 --> 00:00:34.006
We are here to understand students and
make participation more

00:00:34.006 --> 00:00:34.674
workable.

00:00:34.674 --> 00:00:38.013
We value students as they are and support
their participation

00:00:38.013 --> 00:00:39.025
and belonging.

00:00:39.025 --> 00:00:41.772
You don't need to arrive with all the
answers.

00:00:41.772 --> 00:00:44.816
Bring your questions, your experience and
a willingness to look

00:00:44.816 --> 00:00:45.169
again.

00:00:47.121 --> 00:00:52.043
I acknowledge the Traditional Custodians
of the lands on which we are learning

00:00:52.044 --> 00:00:57.158
today, and pay my respects to Elders past
and present. I recognise the continuing

00:00:57.159 --> 00:01:01.698
connections of Aboriginal and Torres
Strait Islander peoples to Country,

00:01:01.699 --> 00:01:06.493
culture and community. I extend that
respect to Aboriginal and Torres Strait

00:01:06.494 --> 00:01:09.689
Islander people taking part in this
learning today.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.780
Our Statement of Reconciliation is in your
workbook for you to read.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.920
For the sake of this demonstration, I'll
read it aloud.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.880
We at Positive Partnerships value and
recognise the diversity of

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.320
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander
cultures and heritage

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.520
and their proud part in Australia’s
national identity.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:34.530
We acknowledge and continue to learn about
Australia’s colonial

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:39.640
past and the historic and ongoing impacts
on Australia’s First Nations people.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.270
We believe in and strongly support the
Truth-telling of

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.140
Australia’s colonial history

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.850
and in moving forward as a nation we must
encourage open,

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:54.280
honest dialogue and build relationships
built on respect and trust.

00:01:56.681 --> 00:02:01.073
First Nations people are advised that this
workshop may contain images and

00:02:01.074 --> 00:02:03.599
voices of people who are no longer with
us.

00:02:05.601 --> 00:02:10.901
Everyone is welcome here, and you are
welcome to be authentically yourself. Use

00:02:10.902 --> 00:02:16.066
the strategies that help you take part.
You can move, change position, write,

00:02:16.067 --> 00:02:20.891
speak, listen or pass. If there is
something the delivery team can do to

00:02:20.892 --> 00:02:26.192
make participation more accessible, let us
know. Let's begin by getting to know

00:02:26.193 --> 00:02:31.425
the people around us. At your table, share
your first name, your role, and one

00:02:31.426 --> 00:02:36.183
hope you have for today. Keep the
introduction brief so everyone has an

00:02:36.184 --> 00:02:41.619
opportunity. You're also welcome simply to
listen. There is no need to disclose a

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:46.785
diagnosis or share private information
about yourself, a student or a school.

00:02:46.786 --> 00:02:51.950
What would make this learning useful in
your work? Take a moment to introduce

00:02:51.951 --> 00:02:52.968
yourselves now.

00:03:01.001 --> 00:03:05.456
Let's come back together. Keep that hope
in mind as we go. There will be

00:03:05.457 --> 00:03:10.540
opportunities to connect the content with
sensory experience, thinking, the school

00:03:10.541 --> 00:03:14.996
day, learning design and partnership. Your
questions are useful starting

00:03:14.997 --> 00:03:18.321
points, even when they don't have an
immediate answer.

00:03:19.841 --> 00:03:23.604
Before we get into the content, take a
moment to check the practical

00:03:23.605 --> 00:03:28.154
arrangements for your setting: the exits,
bathroom facilities, tea and coffee, and

00:03:28.155 --> 00:03:32.536
any quiet or lower-sensory space that is
available. Keep your Participant Guide

00:03:32.537 --> 00:03:36.917
and something to write with nearby. We'll
use the guide throughout the day, and

00:03:36.918 --> 00:03:41.074
there will be time to provide feedback
through the evaluation. You can also

00:03:41.075 --> 00:03:45.343
write questions down if speaking to the
group isn't your preferred way to ask

00:03:45.344 --> 00:03:49.725
them. Please be considerate with phone
sounds and calls, and follow the venue's

00:03:49.726 --> 00:03:54.107
arrangements for no smoking or vaping.
Attend to your own needs as we work. You

00:03:54.108 --> 00:03:58.151
don't need to wait for a scheduled break
to use a strategy that helps you

00:03:58.152 --> 00:04:01.913
participate. Confirm any local
arrangements with your delivery team.

00:04:03.441 --> 00:04:08.214
Turn to page four of your Participant
Guide. Bring two or three students to

00:04:08.215 --> 00:04:13.181
mind. Choose students whose experiences
differ in some way: perhaps their age,

00:04:13.182 --> 00:04:18.149
how they communicate, or what different
parts of school are like for them. Use

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:22.665
initials or a role description in your
notes. We won't need identifying

00:04:22.666 --> 00:04:27.761
information. Begin with the whole person.
What interests them? What strengths do

00:04:27.762 --> 00:04:32.471
you notice? How do they connect with
people or with the things they enjoy?

00:04:32.472 --> 00:04:37.438
Then ask yourself: what am I here to
understand more deeply about their school

00:04:37.439 --> 00:04:42.148
experience? Keep that as an open question,
rather than deciding the answer

00:04:42.149 --> 00:04:46.793
now. This is private thinking first.
Sharing is optional. We'll return to

00:04:46.794 --> 00:04:51.696
these same students when we map the school
day, consider learning design, and

00:04:51.697 --> 00:04:56.792
reflect on what to carry forward. Give
yourself a moment to choose your students

00:04:56.793 --> 00:04:58.146
and make a few notes.

00:05:06.161 --> 00:05:10.883
Keep those notes available. You don't need
a complete picture yet. The gaps in

00:05:10.884 --> 00:05:15.238
what we know are part of the work. As we
move through the workshop, keep

00:05:15.239 --> 00:05:19.654
distinguishing what a student has
communicated, what you've observed, and

00:05:19.655 --> 00:05:22.412
what you'd still like to understand with
them.

00:05:23.921 --> 00:05:28.677
We're going to begin with student voices.
Listen to each speaker's own account, and

00:05:28.678 --> 00:05:33.261
allow it to stand on its own terms. You
don't need to diagnose, compare speakers

00:05:33.262 --> 00:05:37.554
with students you know, or reduce the
video to one lesson. Just notice what

00:05:37.555 --> 00:05:41.906
stays with you, and what you want to
understand more deeply. We'll have time

00:05:41.907 --> 00:05:43.470
to reflect after the video.

00:08:05.881 --> 00:08:09.781
Take a moment before moving into
discussion. Keep the speakers' own words

00:08:09.782 --> 00:08:12.380
and experiences at the centre of your
reflection.

00:08:15.401 --> 00:08:20.290
Stay on page four of your Participant
Guide. What did you notice? What has

00:08:20.291 --> 00:08:25.314
stayed with you? What would you like to
understand more deeply? Begin with a

00:08:25.315 --> 00:08:30.606
little private writing. Anchor your notes
in what the speakers said, rather than

00:08:30.607 --> 00:08:35.430
making an interpretation from their
appearance or behaviour. Then, if you

00:08:35.431 --> 00:08:40.387
wish, share one reflection with someone
nearby. Try to leave each speaker's

00:08:40.388 --> 00:08:45.612
account intact. We don't need to reach
agreement about a single message, and we

00:08:45.613 --> 00:08:50.502
don't need to compare a speaker with one
particular child. Make room for a

00:08:50.503 --> 00:08:55.459
question that remains open as well as
something that feels clear. Take some

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:57.335
time with those prompts now.

00:09:05.361 --> 00:09:10.177
Let's return. Keep one question from that
reflection where you can see it. We will

00:09:10.178 --> 00:09:14.340
come back to sensory experience, thinking,
communication and the school

00:09:14.341 --> 00:09:18.860
day. Some questions may need further
conversation through the program or your

00:09:18.861 --> 00:09:23.261
local channels. Listening carefully
includes recognising what we still need

00:09:23.262 --> 00:09:23.796
to learn.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:29.968
Let's take a moment with the language on
this slide, because it gives us a shared

00:09:29.968 --> 00:09:34.208
starting point. Neurodiversity is the
whole picture. It includes everyone.

00:09:34.208 --> 00:09:38.914
Neurotypical and neurodivergent describe
brain patterns within that diversity, and

00:09:38.914 --> 00:09:43.271
autism is one expression of it. Here,
neurotypical describes the more common

00:09:43.271 --> 00:09:47.744
brain patterns. Neurodivergent describes
brain patterns that differ from those

00:09:47.744 --> 00:09:52.160
most common patterns. So both sit within
the wider picture of neurodiversity.

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:56.517
Keeping those ideas clear helps us use the
language consistently through the

00:09:56.517 --> 00:10:00.990
day. Now, hold in mind the students you
were thinking about earlier. The words

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:05.521
can help us ask better questions about
their experience, but they won't tell us

00:10:05.521 --> 00:10:09.530
everything about any one of them. We still
need to know that student's

00:10:09.530 --> 00:10:14.003
interests, their ways of communicating,
and what different parts of school are

00:10:14.003 --> 00:10:18.418
like for them. We'll also respect the
language people use for themselves. The

00:10:18.418 --> 00:10:22.834
purpose here is to support dignity and
curiosity. As we go, keep asking: does

00:10:22.834 --> 00:10:26.201
this language help me understand the
person in front of me?

00:10:27.721 --> 00:10:32.326
That brings us to what it means to be
neurodiversity affirming. The starting

00:10:32.327 --> 00:10:36.626
point is that differences are not
deficits. There are different ways of

00:10:36.627 --> 00:10:41.416
knowing, doing, being and belonging, and
everyone has strengths and experiences

00:10:41.417 --> 00:10:46.022
challenges. That means allowing for
different ways of thinking, learning and

00:10:46.023 --> 00:10:50.690
taking part. When we look at belonging,
for instance, we can stay open to the

00:10:50.691 --> 00:10:55.542
ways a student experiences connection,
rather than expecting it to look the same

00:10:55.543 --> 00:11:00.149
for everyone. In a primary classroom, it's
easy for the things that need our

00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:04.816
immediate attention to fill the whole
picture. A difficult transition. A task

00:11:04.817 --> 00:11:09.238
that hasn't started. A moment when joining
the group has become too much.

00:11:09.239 --> 00:11:13.599
Those things matter. We need to understand
them and provide support. And

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:18.451
we need to keep the rest of the person
visible. What interests them? What brings

00:11:18.452 --> 00:11:23.303
them joy? How do they connect? What can
they do that we might miss if we're only

00:11:23.304 --> 00:11:28.216
watching for difficulty? Take one of your
students now. What becomes more visible

00:11:28.217 --> 00:11:32.884
when you include their strengths and
interests in the picture? Just hold that

00:11:32.885 --> 00:11:34.174
thought for a moment.

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:47.441
A strengths-based approach also takes
difficulty seriously. A student can have

00:11:47.442 --> 00:11:52.888
real strengths and still need substantial
support. Their capacity can change with

00:11:52.889 --> 00:11:58.471
the task, the environment and the day. So
our language needs to make room for both.

00:11:58.472 --> 00:12:03.236
We can describe what's becoming difficult,
notice what the situation is

00:12:03.237 --> 00:12:08.479
asking of the student, and consider what
support would help. We begin with the

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:11.270
person, and we stay curious about access.

00:12:12.801 --> 00:12:17.689
The social model helps us put that
curiosity to work. On this slide, we're

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:23.046
using an environment-first lens. We look
for barriers in the place, the task, the

00:12:23.047 --> 00:12:28.136
routine and the expectations. Then we ask
what can change around the student.

00:12:28.137 --> 00:12:33.494
Here's an imaginary classroom example. A
class is gathering for a group activity.

00:12:33.495 --> 00:12:38.651
The usual expectation is that everyone
sits close together, in the same space,

00:12:38.652 --> 00:12:43.673
for the whole activity. One student finds
that arrangement difficult. Before

00:12:43.674 --> 00:12:48.629
deciding what the student needs to do
differently, we can look at what this

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:54.054
routine asks of them. How crowded is the
space? What choices are there about where

00:12:54.055 --> 00:12:58.876
to sit? Is sitting in that particular
place necessary to take part in the

00:12:58.877 --> 00:13:04.099
activity? We might discuss another place
to sit, or a different way of joining,

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:09.189
with the student and teacher. Those are
possibilities to investigate with the

00:13:09.190 --> 00:13:14.480
student. We haven't established what will
help just by coming up with an option.

00:13:14.481 --> 00:13:19.704
The next two steps on the slide matter:
include the student's communication and

00:13:19.705 --> 00:13:25.061
experience, and review whether the change
makes participation more workable. What

00:13:25.062 --> 00:13:30.285
can the student tell or show us about the
change? Is it helping them take part?

00:13:30.286 --> 00:13:35.174
Changing an environment won't make every
difficulty disappear. It gives us

00:13:35.175 --> 00:13:40.531
somewhere useful to begin, with barriers
and supports that we can notice, discuss

00:13:40.532 --> 00:13:41.268
and review.

00:13:42.801 --> 00:13:47.253
Let's widen that question to school
itself. You can use the space on page

00:13:47.254 --> 00:13:52.016
five of your Participant Guide as you
think. What is school for? Who is school

00:13:52.017 --> 00:13:56.346
for? How do students learn? And whose ways
of communicating, moving and

00:13:56.347 --> 00:14:01.170
participating are treated as expected?
These are big questions. There isn't one

00:14:01.171 --> 00:14:06.056
correct answer we're trying to arrive at.
They're an invitation to look again at

00:14:06.057 --> 00:14:10.757
familiar routines and the assumptions
inside them. Think about a routine in a

00:14:10.758 --> 00:14:15.334
setting where you work: perhaps arriving
in the morning, joining a group or

00:14:15.335 --> 00:14:19.911
showing what you've learnt. What does that
routine seem to assume about how

00:14:19.912 --> 00:14:24.736
students will communicate or participate?
What do we notice and encourage? What

00:14:24.737 --> 00:14:29.560
might be harder to see? This is a chance
to examine the routine with curiosity.

00:14:29.561 --> 00:14:34.261
Every classroom has patterns that have
become familiar. Looking at them again

00:14:34.262 --> 00:14:38.838
can open up choices. We can value the care
and effort that educators bring,

00:14:38.839 --> 00:14:43.353
while still asking whether a familiar
expectation is helping every student

00:14:43.354 --> 00:14:48.116
access the learning. For a moment, work
quietly on your own. Choose one of the

00:14:48.117 --> 00:14:52.446
four questions that catches your
attention, and jot down a thought or a

00:14:52.447 --> 00:14:54.362
question you'd like to explore.

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:07.093
Hold on to the question you've chosen. Our
assumptions influence what we notice,

00:15:07.094 --> 00:15:11.489
what we reward and what we think needs to
change. We'll now take that wider

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:16.064
reflection into one specific routine, so
the question becomes something we can

00:15:16.065 --> 00:15:17.074
examine together.

00:15:18.601 --> 00:15:23.685
Now turn to a small group near you. To
begin, take the question about whose ways

00:15:23.686 --> 00:15:27.934
of communicating, moving and participating
are treated as expected.

00:15:27.935 --> 00:15:32.826
Choose one familiar routine or decision to
explore together. Keep it specific

00:15:32.827 --> 00:15:37.846
enough to picture. You might choose how
students join a whole-class discussion,

00:15:37.847 --> 00:15:42.674
for example. Talk about the routine and
the system, without naming students,

00:15:42.675 --> 00:15:47.824
colleagues or schools. Then work through
the prompts on screen. Which assumptions

00:15:47.825 --> 00:15:52.458
shape that routine? Who can access it
easily? Where might the environment

00:15:52.459 --> 00:15:57.222
create a barrier? How could curiosity
change the next response? If you find

00:15:57.223 --> 00:16:01.792
yourselves focusing mainly on what a
student does, come back to what the

00:16:01.793 --> 00:16:06.169
environment, task or expectation is
contributing. Before we come back

00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:10.804
together, choose one concise observation
to share, and one example of how

00:16:10.805 --> 00:16:15.503
curiosity could change your next response.
Keep the prompts in view as you

00:16:15.504 --> 00:16:16.789
talk. You can begin.

00:16:24.800 --> 00:16:29.566
Let's come back together. Finish the
thought you're on, and keep your notes.

00:16:29.567 --> 00:16:34.333
If you're sharing with the group, offer
one concise observation: one example

00:16:34.334 --> 00:16:39.354
where curiosity could change your next
response. Keep the example focused on the

00:16:39.355 --> 00:16:44.375
routine, the task or the environment. As
we close, keep that question about your

00:16:44.376 --> 00:16:49.142
next response with you. The shift we're
practising is from assumptions about

00:16:49.143 --> 00:16:53.718
students towards questions about access,
environment and learning design.

00:16:53.719 --> 00:16:58.358
Curiosity gives us time to look at the
task and the setting before placing

00:16:58.359 --> 00:17:03.379
another demand on a student. It helps us
identify something to explore, with the

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:08.400
student's experience still at the centre.
Keep your example available as we move

00:17:08.401 --> 00:17:08.591
on.

00:17:10.120 --> 00:17:15.582
The Disability Standards for Education
2005 are part of the context for our work

00:17:15.583 --> 00:17:20.906
on access and adjustments. There are three
Australian Government Department of

00:17:20.907 --> 00:17:25.815
Education resources to be aware of
alongside this workshop. The first is

00:17:25.816 --> 00:17:31.347
Getting started. The second is Advocating
with and for your child: A workbook for

00:17:31.348 --> 00:17:36.671
parents and carers. That workbook is
written for families, so it is useful for

00:17:36.672 --> 00:17:42.203
educators to know what parents and carers
may be reading and working through. The

00:17:42.204 --> 00:17:47.182
third resource is Milestones and
transitions. The links are listed in the

00:17:47.183 --> 00:17:52.783
facilitator resources. Use the published
materials to follow up, and take detailed

00:17:52.784 --> 00:17:58.383
or school-specific questions through the
appropriate program or local channel. For

00:17:58.384 --> 00:18:03.431
our work today, keep the connection with
access in view: understanding the

00:18:03.432 --> 00:18:08.548
student, looking closely at the demands of
the setting, and considering the

00:18:08.549 --> 00:18:12.766
adjustments and support that make
participation more workable.

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:19.387
Autism can be understood as difference and
as a dynamic disability. Holding both

00:18:19.388 --> 00:18:24.300
ideas matters. Recognising difference and
strengths does not mean overlooking

00:18:24.301 --> 00:18:29.150
difficulty or the need for substantial
support. Access and support needs can

00:18:29.151 --> 00:18:34.193
change with the task, the environment,
relationships and the capacity available

00:18:34.194 --> 00:18:39.430
at that time. Something a student can do
in one setting, or on one day, may not be

00:18:39.431 --> 00:18:44.537
available in another. We need to look at
what is happening around the student as

00:18:44.538 --> 00:18:49.774
well as what the task is asking of them.
That takes us away from a fixed judgement

00:18:49.775 --> 00:18:54.623
about what someone can always do. It
invites a more useful question: what is

00:18:54.624 --> 00:18:59.472
making participation possible here, and
what support is needed now? Keep the

00:18:59.473 --> 00:19:04.321
individual in view. There isn't one
pattern that will explain every Autistic

00:19:04.322 --> 00:19:09.041
student's experience. Strengths,
difficulties and fluctuating capacity all

00:19:09.042 --> 00:19:13.955
belong in the picture we build with the
student and the people who know them.

00:19:15.481 --> 00:19:20.113
Here is where the day is taking us. We'll
develop a neurodiversity affirming

00:19:20.114 --> 00:19:24.622
approach, deepen our understanding of
Autistic experience, notice how that

00:19:24.623 --> 00:19:29.440
experience meets the school day, consider
accessible learning environments, and

00:19:29.441 --> 00:19:34.011
work in partnership. The first two
sessions build our shared understanding.

00:19:34.012 --> 00:19:38.829
Then we'll bring that understanding into a
school day, in primary and secondary

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:43.400
settings, and into practical choices about
the support you give. Belonging,

00:19:43.401 --> 00:19:48.403
capacity and joy stay with us throughout.
The workshop schedule allows for morning

00:19:48.404 --> 00:19:53.098
tea from ten forty-five to eleven, and
lunch from twelve thirty to one. Treat

00:19:53.099 --> 00:19:58.039
those as the planned timings and follow
the arrangements for your session. Before

00:19:58.040 --> 00:20:03.043
we move on, turn to My starting point on
page five of your Participant Guide. Make

00:20:03.044 --> 00:20:08.046
a brief note of where you are beginning: a
question, a hope or an idea you want to

00:20:08.047 --> 00:20:12.864
examine. We will return to this in our
final reflection. Take a moment to write

00:20:12.865 --> 00:20:13.110
now.

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:25.542
Keep that starting point. You don't have
to resolve it yet. Next we'll explore

00:20:25.543 --> 00:20:30.138
sensory experience, thinking and internal
signals, using the students you have in

00:20:30.139 --> 00:20:33.240
mind to connect the ideas with your own
school context.

00:20:39.761 --> 00:20:44.479
Let's return to the students you brought
to mind. In this session we'll explore

00:20:44.480 --> 00:20:49.016
sensory processing, Autistic thinking and
internal signals. You can find the

00:20:49.017 --> 00:20:53.311
session summary on page six of your
Participant Guide. We are building a

00:20:53.312 --> 00:20:57.908
fuller picture of each student. These
ideas give us areas for curiosity; they

00:20:57.909 --> 00:21:02.385
are not a checklist for deciding what a
student must be like. Keep noticing

00:21:02.386 --> 00:21:07.164
strengths and interests, the environment,
the demands of a task, and the support

00:21:07.165 --> 00:21:11.822
that may help. We'll look at these areas
separately so we can understand them,

00:21:11.823 --> 00:21:16.359
then begin putting them together again. In
the next session we'll carry that

00:21:16.360 --> 00:21:20.715
understanding into communication,
connection and the everyday routines of

00:21:20.716 --> 00:21:21.137
school.

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:27.434
We'll start with sensory experience: how
the world arrives. Before we ask a

00:21:27.435 --> 00:21:32.359
student to join a conversation, begin a
task or make sense of an instruction,

00:21:32.360 --> 00:21:37.349
there is already a sensory environment
around them. That environment can bring

00:21:37.350 --> 00:21:42.210
pleasure, interest and connection, as well
as difficulty. Keep both in view.

00:21:42.211 --> 00:21:47.005
We're interested in sensory joy and
authentic expression alongside overload

00:21:47.006 --> 00:21:52.060
and barriers. As we work, remember that an
observation can open a question, but

00:21:52.061 --> 00:21:56.855
it cannot tell us everything about someone
else's internal experience. Stay

00:21:56.856 --> 00:21:59.512
curious about the person and the context.

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:05.573
What do you understand by sensory
processing? Before we settle on a shared

00:22:05.574 --> 00:22:10.543
definition, take a moment to put it into
your own words. Think about what happens

00:22:10.544 --> 00:22:15.574
between information arriving through the
senses and the response you might notice.

00:22:23.600 --> 00:22:29.212
For this workshop, sensory processing is
the way the brain receives, processes and

00:22:29.213 --> 00:22:34.687
responds to information from the senses.
There are three parts to hold together:

00:22:34.688 --> 00:22:39.746
receiving information, making sense of it,
and responding. We'll look more

00:22:39.747 --> 00:22:44.875
closely at that sequence in a moment.
First, let's widen the picture of the

00:22:44.876 --> 00:22:46.122
senses themselves.

00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:52.785
Many of us learnt five senses: touch,
vision, taste, hearing and smell. This

00:22:52.786 --> 00:22:58.000
workshop uses eight. The other three are
proprioception, vestibular sense and

00:22:58.001 --> 00:23:03.557
interoception. Touch includes information
about texture, pressure and temperature.

00:23:03.558 --> 00:23:08.292
Vision gives information about things such
as colour, shape, depth and

00:23:08.293 --> 00:23:13.712
position. Gustatory and oral experience
includes taste, texture, temperature and

00:23:13.713 --> 00:23:18.584
the sensation of chewing. Hearing includes
pitch, loudness, distance and

00:23:18.585 --> 00:23:22.975
familiar sounds. Smell can also be closely
connected with memory.

00:23:22.976 --> 00:23:28.395
Proprioception gives us information about
where our body is in space, drawing on

00:23:28.396 --> 00:23:33.541
signals from our muscles and joints.
Pressure or movement may be something a

00:23:33.542 --> 00:23:38.482
student seeks for that input. The
vestibular sense relates to balance and

00:23:38.483 --> 00:23:43.353
movement, including the speed and
direction of head movement. Rocking or

00:23:43.354 --> 00:23:48.774
spinning may serve a regulating purpose
for some students. Interoception relates

00:23:48.775 --> 00:23:54.125
to signals from inside the body. We'll
return to it later in the session. These

00:23:54.126 --> 00:23:59.546
senses work together. A single classroom
experience can involve several at once.

00:23:59.547 --> 00:24:04.417
The three less familiar senses can be
especially useful to consider when

00:24:04.418 --> 00:24:09.495
something about a student's experience
isn't yet clear to us. Keep these as

00:24:09.496 --> 00:24:14.641
possibilities to explore, rather than
explanations we can assign by watching

00:24:14.642 --> 00:24:19.650
alone. Students will differ. There is also
an interoception webinar in the

00:24:19.651 --> 00:24:23.490
Positive Partnerships resources if you
want to follow up.

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:29.198
Here is the sequence again: sensory
information, processing, response.

00:24:29.199 --> 00:24:33.214
Imagine you're wearing a jumper and
becoming hot. Information about

00:24:33.215 --> 00:24:38.021
temperature arrives. You make sense of
that information, including what it means

00:24:38.022 --> 00:24:42.767
in relation to your experience, and you
might respond by taking the jumper off.

00:24:42.768 --> 00:24:46.905
In a classroom, this processing helps us
keep track of our bodies and

00:24:46.906 --> 00:24:51.530
surroundings, notice what matters and
direct attention. It is part of safety,

00:24:51.531 --> 00:24:56.459
learning and engagement. There is a great
deal happening before a response becomes

00:24:56.460 --> 00:25:01.266
visible to someone else. That is why we
need to look beyond the behaviour we can

00:25:01.267 --> 00:25:06.012
see. Sensory processing is not simply a
deliberate choice a student makes about

00:25:06.013 --> 00:25:10.211
how the world affects them. When we notice
a response, we can ask what

00:25:10.212 --> 00:25:14.714
information is arriving, what the
environment is asking of the student, and

00:25:14.715 --> 00:25:16.965
what we need to understand with them.

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:23.472
We're going to watch two short clips. The
first is an animation that explores

00:25:23.473 --> 00:25:28.531
sensory overwhelm through images and
sound. The next offers a personal account

00:25:28.532 --> 00:25:33.327
of sensory joy. Both belong in our
understanding of sensory experience. As

00:25:33.328 --> 00:25:37.991
you watch the animation, notice what it
draws your attention to. You may

00:25:37.992 --> 00:25:43.115
recognise something, or you may encounter
a portrayal that is unfamiliar. Leave

00:25:43.116 --> 00:25:48.108
room for either response, and remember
that no single portrayal can represent

00:25:48.109 --> 00:25:53.101
every Autistic person's experience. We'll
leave a quiet moment after the clip

00:25:53.102 --> 00:25:54.284
before continuing.

00:28:21.520 --> 00:28:26.481
Take a moment with what you've seen and
heard. The animation gives us a way to

00:28:26.482 --> 00:28:31.378
consider sensory overwhelm, while each
person's experience remains their own.

00:28:31.379 --> 00:28:36.147
Keep any questions it raises with you.
We'll now make room for sensory joy.

00:28:39.160 --> 00:28:44.700
In this next clip, we'll hear a personal
account of sensory joy. As you listen,

00:28:44.701 --> 00:28:49.886
consider what becomes visible when we
include pleasure, connection and the

00:28:49.887 --> 00:28:53.509
richness of sensory experience in our
understanding.

00:29:43.000 --> 00:29:48.757
Sensory experience can include intensity,
depth and pleasure as well as difficulty.

00:29:48.758 --> 00:29:54.305
The same person can experience both joy
and overwhelm. For example, music may be

00:29:54.306 --> 00:29:59.432
a source of enjoyment for someone, while a
different sound or situation is

00:29:59.433 --> 00:30:04.277
difficult. That is an illustration, rather
than a conclusion about any

00:30:04.278 --> 00:30:09.544
individual speaker. Keep asking what
brings this particular student joy, and

00:30:09.545 --> 00:30:11.791
how school can make room for it.

00:30:13.320 --> 00:30:18.375
Sensory joy and stimming belong in this
conversation. Stimming can be part of

00:30:18.376 --> 00:30:23.165
regulation, pleasure, connection and
authentic expression. Our task is to

00:30:23.166 --> 00:30:28.553
understand what it means for the student,
rather than treating it as disruption by

00:30:28.554 --> 00:30:32.744
default. Notice what brings joy. Recognise
the student's ways of

00:30:32.745 --> 00:30:37.800
regulating and expressing themselves.
Avoid making harmless stimming a target

00:30:37.801 --> 00:30:42.590
simply because it looks different from
what is expected. And ask what the

00:30:42.591 --> 00:30:47.513
student would like other people to
understand. Think about one student now.

00:30:47.514 --> 00:30:52.502
Where is sensory joy visible in their
school day? What opportunities do they

00:30:52.503 --> 00:30:57.425
have to enjoy, explore or regulate in ways
that work for them? Make a brief

00:30:57.426 --> 00:31:01.616
note of something you know, and something
you would like to ask.

00:31:09.640 --> 00:31:14.172
Keep those two things distinct: what you
already know from the student, and what

00:31:14.173 --> 00:31:18.131
you still need to explore. Sensory support
is about more than reducing

00:31:18.132 --> 00:31:22.319
difficulty. It can also make space for
pleasure and for ways of being that

00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:23.524
matter to the person.

00:31:25.040 --> 00:31:29.526
This slide introduces four sensory
response patterns from occupational

00:31:29.527 --> 00:31:34.142
therapist Winnie Dunn's framework. You may
encounter this language in an

00:31:34.143 --> 00:31:39.279
occupational therapy report or a Sensory
Profile School Companion questionnaire.

00:31:39.280 --> 00:31:44.351
The patterns are useful lenses, rather
than fixed types of person. Registration

00:31:44.352 --> 00:31:49.292
describes a pattern of noticing less
sensory information or missing cues that

00:31:49.293 --> 00:31:54.494
others notice. Seeking describes actively
looking for more input, perhaps through

00:31:54.495 --> 00:31:59.501
movement, touch or sound. Sensitivity
describes noticing sensory input readily

00:31:59.502 --> 00:32:04.183
and being affected by it, without
necessarily taking action to reduce it.

00:32:04.184 --> 00:32:09.254
Avoiding describes actively trying to
reduce input, perhaps by moving away from

00:32:09.255 --> 00:32:13.741
a crowd or covering the ears. The slide
also uses under-responsive and

00:32:13.742 --> 00:32:18.293
over-responsive language, and
distinguishes more passive responses from

00:32:18.294 --> 00:32:22.974
active ways of changing the input. Keep
that distinction connected to the

00:32:22.975 --> 00:32:27.526
student's experience, rather than treating
the labels as a judgement. A

00:32:27.527 --> 00:32:32.468
student may show different patterns for
different senses. Their responses may

00:32:32.469 --> 00:32:36.889
also change with the context, the load and
the day. We are looking at

00:32:36.890 --> 00:32:42.026
nervous-system responses, not simply a
decision to cooperate or avoid a task. So

00:32:42.027 --> 00:32:46.643
use the framework to sharpen your
questions. What input is present? What

00:32:46.644 --> 00:32:51.194
has the student communicated? What pattern
might be worth exploring? An

00:32:51.195 --> 00:32:56.071
observation gives us a starting point, not
certainty about what is happening

00:32:56.072 --> 00:32:56.526
inside.

00:32:58.040 --> 00:33:03.268
Let's apply that to a classroom routine.
Turn to the sensory activity on page

00:33:03.269 --> 00:33:08.635
seven of your Participant Guide, and work
in a small group. Choose one familiar

00:33:08.636 --> 00:33:13.795
routine that you can describe without
identifying a student or school. Start

00:33:13.796 --> 00:33:18.130
with the sensory demands of the
environment and the task, before

00:33:18.131 --> 00:33:23.428
discussing a student's response. What
might someone need to take in, filter or

00:33:23.429 --> 00:33:27.762
manage? You could consider sound,
lighting, movement, proximity,

00:33:27.763 --> 00:33:33.267
transitions or materials. These are areas
to investigate, not a universal list of

00:33:33.268 --> 00:33:38.633
triggers. Next, consider possible ways to
offer access or choice. How could the

00:33:38.634 --> 00:33:44.138
student communicate what works for them?
Treat each idea as a question to explore

00:33:44.139 --> 00:33:49.711
with the student, rather than a solution
you've already established. This is not a

00:33:49.712 --> 00:33:54.733
simulation of being Autistic. It is a
review of what the routine asks of a

00:33:54.734 --> 00:33:59.756
person. Choose the routine, map its
demands, then identify an option and a

00:33:59.757 --> 00:34:03.333
question you want to investigate. Begin
together now.

00:34:11.360 --> 00:34:16.476
Let's come back. Look at the option and
question your group has recorded. What

00:34:16.477 --> 00:34:21.261
could you change, offer or investigate
before increasing the demands on a

00:34:21.262 --> 00:34:26.046
student? Keep the sequence in mind:
examine the environment and the task,

00:34:26.047 --> 00:34:31.363
include the student's communication, and
review what actually helps. The point is

00:34:31.364 --> 00:34:36.680
to develop a more precise question about
access, with room for different students

00:34:36.681 --> 00:34:38.340
to need different things.

00:34:39.880 --> 00:34:44.500
We've been considering how the world
arrives through the senses. Now we'll

00:34:44.501 --> 00:34:48.804
turn to how the mind works with
information and where attention goes.

00:34:48.805 --> 00:34:53.804
Keep your students in mind as we explore
detail-first processing, deep focus and

00:34:53.805 --> 00:34:58.678
executive functioning. These concepts
won't describe every Autistic student in

00:34:58.679 --> 00:35:03.235
the same way. Use them to open inquiry,
and keep connecting them with the

00:35:03.236 --> 00:35:05.513
particular person, task and context.

00:35:07.040 --> 00:35:12.412
A detail-first approach offers one way of
understanding Autistic thinking. Top-down

00:35:12.413 --> 00:35:17.457
processing uses expectations and the wider
picture to help filter information.

00:35:17.458 --> 00:35:22.371
Bottom-up processing begins with the
details and builds towards the whole. A

00:35:22.372 --> 00:35:27.743
detail-first account places more emphasis
on that detailed information arriving and

00:35:27.744 --> 00:35:32.854
being worked through. That can help us
consider both depth and load. Noticing a

00:35:32.855 --> 00:35:38.226
change, an inconsistency or a pattern can
be a strength. Processing a great deal of

00:35:38.227 --> 00:35:42.813
detail can also make a situation
demanding. Neither conclusion tells us

00:35:42.814 --> 00:35:47.858
exactly what a particular student is
experiencing. The strengths on this slide

00:35:47.859 --> 00:35:52.903
include pattern recognition, systems
thinking, deep memory, logical precision,

00:35:52.904 --> 00:35:57.620
honesty and directness, depth of
connection, and intense engagement. Take

00:35:57.621 --> 00:36:02.862
each seriously as something valuable in
itself. Strengths are part of a student's

00:36:02.863 --> 00:36:07.710
present identity and learning, rather than
compensation for a difficulty or

00:36:07.711 --> 00:36:12.034
only future potential. You might recognise
connections with sensory

00:36:12.035 --> 00:36:16.621
intensity, literal language or deep focus.
Keep these as connections to

00:36:16.622 --> 00:36:21.666
explore, rather than assuming a thinking
process from behaviour alone. Turn to

00:36:21.667 --> 00:36:26.907
Strengths I can see on page eight of your
Participant Guide. For the students you

00:36:26.908 --> 00:36:31.756
have in mind, note the strengths you
recognise. What evidence supports that

00:36:31.757 --> 00:36:36.735
understanding? What might you be missing
if your attention is mainly drawn to

00:36:36.736 --> 00:36:37.456
difficulty?

00:36:45.480 --> 00:36:50.013
Keep those strengths visible as we
continue. They can help us recognise what

00:36:50.014 --> 00:36:54.003
a student brings to learning and
connection, while still taking the

00:36:54.004 --> 00:36:55.696
support they need seriously.

00:36:57.200 --> 00:37:01.756
Think for a moment about your own
attention. Does it move across many

00:37:01.757 --> 00:37:06.648
topics, or settle deeply into one? That
question introduces monotropism: a

00:37:06.649 --> 00:37:11.876
framework for understanding attention
focused deeply on fewer things at a time.

00:37:11.877 --> 00:37:17.103
Dinah Murray, Mike Lesser and Wenn Lawson
developed the framework, and Autistic

00:37:17.104 --> 00:37:22.196
community voices have continued to extend
it. One image is a torch beam and a

00:37:22.197 --> 00:37:27.691
floodlight. A floodlight spreads across a
wide area; a torch illuminates a narrower

00:37:27.692 --> 00:37:32.718
area intensely. Another is a river with
many channels compared with one deep

00:37:32.719 --> 00:37:37.610
channel. These are ways to picture
different patterns of attention, rather

00:37:37.611 --> 00:37:42.770
than a test for sorting people. Deep
interests can provide a strong route into

00:37:42.771 --> 00:37:48.132
learning, memory and joy. Flow, that full
absorption in an activity, is something

00:37:48.133 --> 00:37:53.426
worth protecting. It is more useful to
recognise that engagement than dismiss it

00:37:53.427 --> 00:37:58.653
as an obsession. Now picture a student
absorbed in a construction task, drawing

00:37:58.654 --> 00:38:03.813
or researching space. Asking them to stop
for recess may require a substantial

00:38:03.814 --> 00:38:08.438
shift. Tendril theory offers another
image: attention reaching into an

00:38:08.439 --> 00:38:13.732
activity, then needing time to withdraw
before moving elsewhere. Transitions can

00:38:13.733 --> 00:38:19.227
have a real cost. Warning and scaffolding
may help make that shift more manageable.

00:38:19.228 --> 00:38:23.985
Keep asking what support fits this student
and this moment. Detail-first

00:38:23.986 --> 00:38:28.811
processing helps us consider how
information arrives; deep focus helps us

00:38:28.812 --> 00:38:30.821
consider where attention goes.

00:38:32.360 --> 00:38:37.695
Executive functioning gives us another set
of questions about the demands of the

00:38:37.696 --> 00:38:42.963
school day. Let's name the ten areas on
the slide. Planning is working out what

00:38:42.964 --> 00:38:47.894
needs to happen. Organisation is arranging
materials and information. Time

00:38:47.895 --> 00:38:52.487
management involves judging and using
time. Working memory helps hold

00:38:52.488 --> 00:38:57.485
information in mind while we use it.
Metacognition means thinking about our

00:38:57.486 --> 00:39:02.348
own thinking. Emotional regulation
concerns managing emotional responses.

00:39:02.349 --> 00:39:07.414
Flexibility involves adapting when
something changes. Attention is directing

00:39:07.415 --> 00:39:12.209
and sustaining focus. Task initiation is
getting started. Persistence is

00:39:12.210 --> 00:39:17.612
continuing with a task. Now consider an
ordinary classroom activity. What does it

00:39:17.613 --> 00:39:22.948
ask a student to plan, start, remember,
sequence, organise, switch and complete?

00:39:22.949 --> 00:39:27.879
A seemingly simple instruction can contain
several demands. Interruptions,

00:39:27.880 --> 00:39:32.810
timing and the way instructions are
presented can change what is required.

00:39:32.811 --> 00:39:38.280
These terms are lenses for inquiry, not a
checklist to apply to a student. A delay

00:39:38.281 --> 00:39:43.684
in starting does not tell us, by itself,
that someone lacks effort or motivation.

00:39:43.685 --> 00:39:48.885
Useful support responds to the specific
demand in the specific context. As you

00:39:48.886 --> 00:39:54.288
look across the ten areas, consider where
each appears in the school day for your

00:39:54.289 --> 00:39:59.151
students, and where task design, timing or
support might make access more

00:39:59.152 --> 00:39:59.759
workable.

00:40:01.280 --> 00:40:05.604
Let's take getting started as one example.
The slide connects it with

00:40:05.605 --> 00:40:10.501
detail-first processing: starting can be
hard when the whole task is not clear

00:40:10.502 --> 00:40:15.144
yet. Some students may not be able to
identify the first step they need to

00:40:15.145 --> 00:40:19.342
take. That gives us a more useful question
than simply repeating an

00:40:19.343 --> 00:40:24.493
instruction to begin. Is the task clear?
Is its purpose and shape available to the

00:40:24.494 --> 00:40:29.263
student? Can they identify a first step?
Those questions don't establish the

00:40:29.264 --> 00:40:33.525
reason for a particular student's
difficulty. They give us something

00:40:33.526 --> 00:40:38.231
specific to check with them. Support can
then respond to the demand we have

00:40:38.232 --> 00:40:41.729
understood, rather than an assumption
about willingness.

00:40:43.240 --> 00:40:47.771
Switching focus gives us a second example.
It can be difficult for some

00:40:47.772 --> 00:40:52.756
students to stop what they are doing and
move their attention elsewhere. Think

00:40:52.757 --> 00:40:58.001
back to the student deeply absorbed in an
activity when recess begins. Transitions

00:40:58.002 --> 00:41:02.856
and unexpected interruptions can be
significant because attention is already

00:41:02.857 --> 00:41:08.036
deeply invested. The slide uses the image
of tunnel vision for the mind. It helps

00:41:08.037 --> 00:41:12.697
us consider the effort involved in
disengaging and redirecting attention.

00:41:12.698 --> 00:41:17.747
Keep the question practical: what is the
transition asking of this student, and

00:41:17.748 --> 00:41:22.797
what warning or scaffolding could make it
more manageable? A timetable tells us

00:41:22.798 --> 00:41:27.524
when the next activity starts. It doesn't,
by itself, tell us what someone

00:41:27.525 --> 00:41:29.725
needs in order to make that shift.

00:41:31.240 --> 00:41:35.945
A young person's account of deep focus and
transitions is planned for this point

00:41:35.946 --> 00:41:40.413
in the workshop, but the clip is not
included in the supplied materials yet.

00:41:40.414 --> 00:41:44.821
Hearing directly from Autistic people
remains central to this learning. The

00:41:44.822 --> 00:41:49.527
frameworks we've introduced can help us
form questions; they cannot stand in for

00:41:49.528 --> 00:41:53.816
a person's own account. Keep that
distinction as we move into discussion.

00:41:53.817 --> 00:41:58.165
What do you know from the students
themselves about their interests, their

00:41:58.166 --> 00:42:02.693
focus and the support they find useful?
Where do you still need to listen and

00:42:02.694 --> 00:42:03.050
learn?

00:42:04.560 --> 00:42:09.695
Turn to the thinking discussion on page
eight, and work with a partner. Hold your

00:42:09.696 --> 00:42:14.832
students in mind as you explore the four
questions on screen. What deep interests

00:42:14.833 --> 00:42:19.262
do they have? What do you notice when they
are fully engaged, and does

00:42:19.263 --> 00:42:24.206
engagement look the same for each of them?
When in the school day do they have

00:42:24.207 --> 00:42:28.828
trouble getting started or shifting
attention? And have you observed them

00:42:28.829 --> 00:42:33.772
fully absorbed in something at school?
What were they doing? Include strengths

00:42:33.773 --> 00:42:38.716
and flow alongside the parts that are
difficult. Separate what a task requires

00:42:38.717 --> 00:42:43.724
from what you observe a student doing, and
leave their internal experience open

00:42:43.725 --> 00:42:48.925
unless they have communicated it. If you
want to extend the discussion, choose one

00:42:48.926 --> 00:42:54.189
task and describe it neutrally. List what
it asks someone to notice, start, hold in

00:42:54.190 --> 00:42:59.133
mind, switch, sequence and complete. Mark
where the environment or instruction

00:42:59.134 --> 00:43:03.948
might reduce unnecessary demand, then
write one question to explore with the

00:43:03.949 --> 00:43:08.379
student or team. Begin with the four
questions, and leave time to note

00:43:08.380 --> 00:43:10.690
something you want to carry forward.

00:43:18.720 --> 00:43:23.331
Let's come back together. If you're
sharing, choose one brief observation or

00:43:23.332 --> 00:43:27.698
question from your discussion. Keep it
connected with a specific student

00:43:27.699 --> 00:43:32.556
experience or task, without identifying
the student. The purpose is to move from

00:43:32.557 --> 00:43:36.983
understanding a concept to recognising
something worth exploring in daily

00:43:36.984 --> 00:43:41.596
learning. Deep interests, starting,
shifting and flow can all help us notice

00:43:41.597 --> 00:43:46.208
more carefully. We can work with the
student's attention and strengths while

00:43:46.209 --> 00:43:51.128
considering the support they need. Keep
those notes for our next session, when we

00:43:51.129 --> 00:43:54.756
connect this thinking with communication
and the school day.

00:43:56.280 --> 00:44:00.893
The final part of this session turns
inward. We named interoception among the

00:44:00.894 --> 00:44:05.507
eight senses; now we'll return to it more
closely. What is happening inside a

00:44:05.508 --> 00:44:10.242
person may not be visible to an observer.
A student can be having an experience

00:44:10.243 --> 00:44:14.735
that we do not recognise from the surface.
Keep that in mind as we consider

00:44:14.736 --> 00:44:18.802
body signals, emotions and the ways we
build understanding together.

00:44:20.320 --> 00:44:25.271
Interoception is about understanding our
body's internal signals. It helps us

00:44:25.272 --> 00:44:30.615
answer the question, how am I doing? Am I
hot or cold? Am I hungry or thirsty? Do I

00:44:30.616 --> 00:44:35.241
need the bathroom? Am I tired? These
signals also contribute to noticing

00:44:35.242 --> 00:44:39.998
changes as we become upset and to
regulation. The other term on this slide

00:44:39.999 --> 00:44:44.885
is alexithymia: difficulty identifying and
describing emotions and feelings.

00:44:44.886 --> 00:44:49.903
Keep that definition clear. Identifying
what is happening and finding words to

00:44:49.904 --> 00:44:54.595
describe it can be difficult. The
questions on screen invite us to notice

00:44:54.596 --> 00:44:59.743
how much interpretation can be involved.
Am I excited or am I anxious? Is crying

00:44:59.744 --> 00:45:04.434
always sadness? What is my friend feeling?
We shouldn't assume the answer

00:45:04.435 --> 00:45:09.126
from outward behaviour. Crying alone, for
example, does not establish one

00:45:09.127 --> 00:45:14.079
particular feeling. And an apparently calm
surface doesn't tell us everything

00:45:14.080 --> 00:45:19.161
about what is happening inside. These
terms help us recognise why understanding

00:45:19.162 --> 00:45:24.049
internal experience matters. They are a
reason to stay open to the student's

00:45:24.050 --> 00:45:29.066
communication and to build understanding
together, rather than to decide their

00:45:29.067 --> 00:45:30.630
internal state for them.

00:45:32.160 --> 00:45:36.855
Sometimes a student experiences a build-up
inside, while the surface gives

00:45:36.856 --> 00:45:41.872
us little indication. If we wait until
distress is obvious, we may miss earlier

00:45:41.873 --> 00:45:46.761
opportunities to respond. The more we
understand a student's early signs, the

00:45:46.762 --> 00:45:51.070
better placed we are to recognise when
support might be useful. That

00:45:51.071 --> 00:45:56.345
understanding needs to be built with each
student, their family and other staff. No

00:45:56.346 --> 00:46:01.105
one person's observation gives us the
whole picture. Think of a shared map:

00:46:01.106 --> 00:46:05.736
what the student communicates, what
different people notice, what remains

00:46:05.737 --> 00:46:10.496
uncertain, and how people can respond
consistently. The map needs to remain

00:46:10.497 --> 00:46:14.999
open to revision as circumstances and
capacity change. An analogy or an

00:46:15.000 --> 00:46:19.115
observation can help start a conversation.
It doesn't replace the

00:46:19.116 --> 00:46:24.326
student's own communication. Keep asking
how the student can contribute, including

00:46:24.327 --> 00:46:29.472
in ways other than a spoken explanation,
and what the environment can do with the

00:46:29.473 --> 00:46:30.951
information they share.

00:46:32.480 --> 00:46:37.685
Turn to Reading the signs on page nine of
your Participant Guide, and work with a

00:46:37.686 --> 00:46:42.436
partner. For the students you have in
mind, what signs might you notice at

00:46:42.437 --> 00:46:47.056
school before they become overwhelmed? Are
those signs the same for each

00:46:47.057 --> 00:46:51.742
student? Use initials or roles in your
notes, and distinguish what you've

00:46:51.743 --> 00:46:56.753
observed from what you think it might
mean. Then consider what would be useful

00:46:56.754 --> 00:47:01.634
for other staff and families to know. What
have they noticed that could help

00:47:01.635 --> 00:47:06.840
you understand the picture? How could the
student communicate their capacity, and

00:47:06.841 --> 00:47:11.526
what could the environment do in response?
You don't need to settle on an

00:47:11.527 --> 00:47:16.211
explanation for every sign. Aim to
identify something useful to share and

00:47:16.212 --> 00:47:21.027
something you still need to explore
together. Take a few minutes with those

00:47:21.028 --> 00:47:21.938
questions now.

00:47:29.960 --> 00:47:34.709
Let's return. Keep one point that would be
useful to discuss with the student,

00:47:34.710 --> 00:47:39.275
their family or other staff. The signs may
differ between students, and the

00:47:39.276 --> 00:47:43.840
meaning of an observation needs to be
checked rather than assumed. A shared

00:47:43.841 --> 00:47:48.467
understanding can help people respond more
consistently and earlier. It also

00:47:48.468 --> 00:47:53.094
gives us a link to our later partnership
work: making room for the student's

00:47:53.095 --> 00:47:57.475
communication, bringing different
perspectives together, and considering

00:47:57.476 --> 00:47:59.634
what support the setting can offer.

00:48:01.160 --> 00:48:06.007
That brings us to morning tea. The
workshop schedule allows a break from ten

00:48:06.008 --> 00:48:10.855
forty-five to eleven; follow the timing
arranged for your session. After the

00:48:10.856 --> 00:48:15.509
break, we'll bring sensory experience,
thinking and internal signals into

00:48:15.510 --> 00:48:20.292
communication, connection and managing
daily capacity. We'll map demand and

00:48:20.293 --> 00:48:25.334
capacity across an ordinary school day,
keeping the same students in mind. Keep

00:48:25.335 --> 00:48:30.182
your notes. The strengths you've named,
the questions about routines and the

00:48:30.183 --> 00:48:34.642
things you still want to understand will
all be useful when we return.

00:48:42.680 --> 00:48:47.586
Welcome back. We're moving now from our
shared understanding of autism into the

00:48:47.586 --> 00:48:52.365
experience of a school day, seen from the
support staff role. You'll find the

00:48:52.365 --> 00:48:57.459
Session Three summary on page ten of your
Participant Guide. Bring back the two or

00:48:57.459 --> 00:49:02.113
three students you've been holding in
mind, with their different strengths,

00:49:02.113 --> 00:49:07.082
preferences and experiences. You may see a
student across classrooms, specialist

00:49:07.082 --> 00:49:11.799
lessons, corridors, breaks and the journey
into or out of school. That close

00:49:11.799 --> 00:49:16.641
view brings valuable observations to
shared planning. It doesn't mean you have

00:49:16.641 --> 00:49:21.044
to interpret, plan or respond alone. We'll
begin with communication and

00:49:21.044 --> 00:49:25.698
connection, then consider what the day
asks of each student and what helps.

00:49:25.698 --> 00:49:29.912
You'll map the day across rooms and
spaces. We'll practise recording

00:49:29.912 --> 00:49:34.440
observations, consider support in the
moment, and audit the moves between

00:49:34.440 --> 00:49:39.282
settings. Those pieces will feed the
support design in Session Four. Keep your

00:49:39.282 --> 00:49:44.377
examples within education practice, leave
out identifying details and stay curious

00:49:44.377 --> 00:49:48.968
about what we don't yet know. The
timetable tells us what happens next. It

00:49:48.968 --> 00:49:52.803
doesn't tell us everything about what it
takes to participate.

00:49:54.321 --> 00:49:59.544
Let’s begin with communication. Speech is
one way of communicating, and we’ll be

00:49:59.545 --> 00:50:04.438
looking at a much wider picture: how
students receive information, how they

00:50:04.439 --> 00:50:09.794
share what matters to them, and the ways
we make both possible. The amount someone

00:50:09.795 --> 00:50:14.820
speaks doesn’t tell us how much they
understand. A student may need different

00:50:14.821 --> 00:50:20.243
ways to understand an instruction, answer
a question, ask for help, request a pause

00:50:20.244 --> 00:50:25.202
or say no. All of those messages matter.
Communication access belongs in the

00:50:25.203 --> 00:50:30.361
design of the classroom and the school
day. We’ll talk about practical options,

00:50:30.362 --> 00:50:35.519
including augmentative and alternative
communication, or A A C. There isn’t one

00:50:35.520 --> 00:50:40.478
approach that suits every student. Follow
the student’s agreed communication

00:50:40.479 --> 00:50:45.570
approach. Take individual assessment or
intervention questions to the teacher,

00:50:45.571 --> 00:50:50.398
the student’s speech pathologist or the
relevant team member. Our focus is

00:50:50.399 --> 00:50:55.622
recognising communication, keeping agreed
supports available and sharing what we

00:50:55.623 --> 00:50:56.085
notice.

00:50:57.601 --> 00:51:02.823
Let’s start with a quick, quiet think.
Since you woke up this morning, how have

00:51:02.824 --> 00:51:08.248
you communicated? See if you can think of
three different ways. Perhaps you spoke,

00:51:08.249 --> 00:51:13.337
sent a message, pointed, showed someone a
photo, moved away, or took a moment

00:51:13.338 --> 00:51:18.226
before answering. Give yourself about
twenty seconds. Then, if you’re with

00:51:18.227 --> 00:51:23.315
colleagues, two or three people might
share one example. Sharing is optional.

00:51:23.316 --> 00:51:27.600
Pause the video now if you’d like that
thinking and sharing time.

00:51:35.601 --> 00:51:41.296
Let’s come back together. We move between
ways of communicating all day, often

00:51:41.297 --> 00:51:45.808
without noticing. Writing, facial
expression, gesture, sign, a

00:51:45.809 --> 00:51:51.725
communication device and speech are all
real communication. One method isn’t more

00:51:51.726 --> 00:51:57.495
valid because it is spoken. That gives us
something useful to notice at school.

00:51:57.496 --> 00:52:02.821
When we say, ‘Use your words,’ are we
overlooking a message a student has

00:52:02.822 --> 00:52:08.516
already communicated? A point, a look, or
moving closer or further away can be

00:52:08.517 --> 00:52:14.508
worth attending to. Our job is to listen
across the ways that person communicates,

00:52:14.509 --> 00:52:18.649
while checking what a message means rather
than assuming.

00:52:20.160 --> 00:52:24.918
There are two parts to keep distinct here:
understanding and expressing.

00:52:24.919 --> 00:52:30.146
Understanding is information coming in and
making sense. That might be a spoken

00:52:30.147 --> 00:52:35.039
instruction, a written sequence, a
demonstration, or a gesture in context.

00:52:35.040 --> 00:52:40.201
Expressing is a message going out: an
idea, a feeling, a question or a choice,

00:52:40.202 --> 00:52:45.429
through any communication method. These
don’t necessarily look the same for one

00:52:45.430 --> 00:52:50.657
person. Someone may speak at length and
still need support to understand spoken

00:52:50.658 --> 00:52:56.020
information. Someone else may understand
much more than they can readily express.

00:52:56.021 --> 00:53:01.449
Neither fluent speech nor limited speech
gives us the whole picture. Communication

00:53:01.450 --> 00:53:06.342
can also change with the person, the
place, the demands and how someone is

00:53:06.343 --> 00:53:11.570
feeling. A A C means augmentative and
alternative communication. It can include

00:53:11.571 --> 00:53:16.933
gesture, signs, a communication board or
book, typing, or a communication device.

00:53:16.934 --> 00:53:21.893
Some people use it alongside speech and
some instead of speech. Repeated or

00:53:21.894 --> 00:53:26.719
scripted words can carry meaning too.
Listen for the context and possible

00:53:26.720 --> 00:53:30.741
purpose before correcting the form. We
don’t need to assign a

00:53:30.742 --> 00:53:35.366
language-processing label before
respecting a student’s communication.

00:53:35.367 --> 00:53:40.326
Individual assessment questions go to the
teacher and relevant team. In the

00:53:40.327 --> 00:53:45.353
settings where we work, the question is:
what does this environment help the

00:53:45.354 --> 00:53:49.509
student understand, and what ways does it
give them to respond?

00:53:51.040 --> 00:53:55.280
We’re going to hear Autistic people
describe communication in their own

00:53:55.280 --> 00:53:59.884
words. As you watch, notice what supports
communication. Choose one idea that

00:53:59.884 --> 00:54:04.427
stands out to you. Let the speakers’
experiences lead your thinking, without

00:54:04.427 --> 00:54:07.758
expecting one account to describe every
Autistic person.

00:59:40.240 --> 00:59:44.740
What stood out to you about what supported
communication? Take a moment to

00:59:44.741 --> 00:59:49.734
choose one idea. You might think about a
communication strength, something another

00:59:49.735 --> 00:59:53.988
person did that helped, or the difference
the environment or available

00:59:53.989 --> 00:59:58.612
communication method made. If you’re
watching with colleagues, invite one or

00:59:58.613 --> 01:00:01.880
two brief responses. Pause here for that
conversation.

01:00:09.880 --> 01:00:14.992
As we come back, hold onto that specific
idea. There is no single Autistic

01:00:14.993 --> 01:00:19.964
communication profile, so we need to
listen to what helps the particular

01:00:19.965 --> 01:00:25.287
person. One practical point to carry into
school is availability. A student’s

01:00:25.288 --> 01:00:30.400
communication system needs to be where
communication happens: in class, at

01:00:30.401 --> 01:00:35.653
break, during transitions and when plans
change. It cannot be limited to one

01:00:35.654 --> 01:00:40.976
activity or a therapy session. Support
staff keep the agreed system available

01:00:40.977 --> 01:00:46.298
and share what they notice with the
teacher and relevant team. We support the

01:00:46.299 --> 01:00:51.691
student’s ways of communicating; we do not
assess or prescribe a system from a

01:00:51.692 --> 01:00:52.882
workshop example.

01:00:54.401 --> 01:00:59.615
Turn to page eleven of your Participant
Guide, or use a sheet of paper. Put one

01:00:59.616 --> 01:01:04.696
of the students you have in mind in the
centre. Around them are home, school,

01:01:04.697 --> 01:01:09.711
friends, community and supports. Who is
part of this student’s communication

01:01:09.712 --> 01:01:14.524
network? Add just one person or place now.
Use roles rather than names: a

01:01:14.525 --> 01:01:19.806
family member, a classmate, a teacher,
someone at a club. You can write, draw or

01:01:19.807 --> 01:01:24.821
use dot points. If it helps, ask a
colleague to scribe your ideas. You don’t

01:01:24.822 --> 01:01:30.103
need to fill every space. Networks can be
small or changing, and an empty box is

01:01:30.104 --> 01:01:35.518
fine. Give yourself about a minute and a
half. Pause the video while you make that

01:01:35.519 --> 01:01:36.520
first addition.

01:01:44.521 --> 01:01:49.337
Keep that map open. You can return to the
rest later. Different people and places

01:01:49.338 --> 01:01:53.792
may reveal different parts of a student’s
communication. A family member, a

01:01:53.793 --> 01:01:57.946
classroom teacher and a support staff
member may each notice something

01:01:57.947 --> 01:02:01.980
valuable. We’re not deciding who
understands the student best. We’re

01:02:01.981 --> 01:02:06.676
recognising that the picture is shared.
Where we know little about part of that

01:02:06.677 --> 01:02:11.372
network, we have a question to explore
with the student and family, rather than

01:02:11.373 --> 01:02:13.359
a gap to fill with an assumption.

01:02:14.881 --> 01:02:19.877
Now let’s think about what should travel
between those people and places, so the

01:02:19.878 --> 01:02:24.494
student doesn’t have to start again with
every new person. We’ll use three

01:02:24.495 --> 01:02:29.049
colours. Purple is a communication
strength. Blue is something that helps

01:02:29.050 --> 01:02:33.729
communication. Orange is something you
would like others to know. Write one

01:02:33.730 --> 01:02:38.600
note, two, or all three. You can use one
of your students, without identifying

01:02:38.601 --> 01:02:43.407
them, or a made-up example. If you don’t
have coloured notes, label the three

01:02:43.408 --> 01:02:48.278
categories on paper. This is also on page
eleven. Allow about three minutes to

01:02:48.279 --> 01:02:53.275
write. Then, if you’re together, spend a
few minutes sharing. You can add a note

01:02:53.276 --> 01:02:58.335
to the board or read one out, and you can
keep your notes private. Leave any note

01:02:58.336 --> 01:03:03.079
with identifying details off the shared
board. Pause now for the writing and

01:03:03.080 --> 01:03:04.151
optional sharing.

01:03:12.161 --> 01:03:16.863
Let’s bring our attention back. Look
across the ideas available to you. What

01:03:16.864 --> 01:03:21.253
similarities do you notice? What strengths
might be missed if we looked

01:03:21.254 --> 01:03:26.269
only for speech? Which supports involve
changing what other people do or provide?

01:03:26.270 --> 01:03:31.098
Think about what would help a relief
teacher, a specialist teacher, playground

01:03:31.099 --> 01:03:36.114
or yard staff, or another support staff
member covering for you. Useful knowledge

01:03:36.115 --> 01:03:40.692
can travel between classrooms, the
playground and home, with the student’s

01:03:40.693 --> 01:03:45.395
and family’s perspectives included. It can
make continuity possible when the

01:03:45.396 --> 01:03:50.412
people around a student change. Keep the
blue notes in view: the things that help

01:03:50.413 --> 01:03:55.301
communication. We’re going to connect
those ideas with three practical options.

01:03:56.841 --> 01:04:01.936
The first option is clear communication.
Use familiar, specific words and give one

01:04:01.937 --> 01:04:06.780
idea or step at a time, in a form and
amount the person can access. That might

01:04:06.781 --> 01:04:11.686
mean making a direction shorter or making
the first step clearer. The second is

01:04:11.687 --> 01:04:15.713
processing time. After giving information,
pause. A person may be

01:04:15.714 --> 01:04:20.682
making sense of what you said, organising
a response and communicating it. If we

01:04:20.683 --> 01:04:25.652
fill every pause with a new version of the
question, we may add more information

01:04:25.653 --> 01:04:30.559
before the earlier information has been
processed. The third is visual support:

01:04:30.560 --> 01:04:35.717
showing information as well as saying it,
so there is something to return to. These

01:04:35.718 --> 01:04:40.624
are options to match to the person and the
moment, rather than a checklist that

01:04:40.625 --> 01:04:45.720
guarantees success. Keep to the student’s
agreed communication approach, and share

01:04:45.721 --> 01:04:50.815
what seems to help with the teacher. Keep
the student’s A A C available and accept

01:04:50.816 --> 01:04:55.596
their preferred ways of responding,
including refusal. A useful communication

01:04:55.597 --> 01:05:00.126
support makes messages easier to
understand and share. It doesn’t require

01:05:00.127 --> 01:05:04.780
the student to produce the answer or
communication style we were expecting.

01:05:06.321 --> 01:05:11.498
Visual supports are information you can
see and come back to. The three examples

01:05:11.499 --> 01:05:16.282
here answer different questions. A timer
can show how much time is left. A

01:05:16.283 --> 01:05:21.329
sequence can show what to do next. A daily
schedule can show what is happening

01:05:21.330 --> 01:05:26.638
today. You may already use a calendar, a
shopping list or a reminder on your phone

01:05:26.639 --> 01:05:31.554
in much the same way. The format needs to
make sense to the person. Objects,

01:05:31.555 --> 01:05:36.404
photographs, written words and symbols can
all be options, on paper or on a

01:05:36.405 --> 01:05:40.467
device. Check that the student understands
what the information

01:05:40.468 --> 01:05:45.448
represents and can reach it when it is
needed. A support displayed across the

01:05:45.449 --> 01:05:50.495
room may exist without being useful at the
moment of need. Think of the visual

01:05:50.496 --> 01:05:55.411
information already available in the
classes and spaces where you work. What

01:05:55.412 --> 01:06:00.589
question does it answer? Could a student
return to it without asking an adult? A

01:06:00.590 --> 01:06:05.242
visual support offers information; it
isn’t a condition the student must

01:06:05.243 --> 01:06:10.289
satisfy to access something they enjoy.
Visual supports and A A C can overlap,

01:06:10.290 --> 01:06:15.598
but they aren’t interchangeable. Keep the
person’s communication methods available

01:06:15.599 --> 01:06:20.710
as well. We’ll return to this in Session
Four when you plan one support moment.

01:06:20.711 --> 01:06:25.691
Keep agreed supports available between
settings and tell the teacher what you

01:06:25.692 --> 01:06:26.150
notice.

01:06:27.681 --> 01:06:32.161
Double empathy asks us to look at
understanding in both directions. When

01:06:32.162 --> 01:06:36.958
people have different experiences and ways
of communicating, it can be harder

01:06:36.959 --> 01:06:41.061
for them to understand one another. That
doesn’t mean every social

01:06:41.062 --> 01:06:46.237
misunderstanding sits inside the Autistic
student. Consider a classroom discussion,

01:06:46.238 --> 01:06:50.781
group task or playground interaction.
Whose expectations are shaping what

01:06:50.782 --> 01:06:55.831
counts as joining in? Who is expected to
adjust? What would make this interaction

01:06:55.832 --> 01:07:00.691
clearer or safer for the people involved?
For example, an adult might expect a

01:07:00.692 --> 01:07:05.046
quick spoken response as a sign of
connection. A student might connect

01:07:05.047 --> 01:07:09.716
through a shared activity, through
detailed talk about an interest, or with

01:07:09.717 --> 01:07:14.135
more time between turns. Those are
possible differences to explore, not

01:07:14.136 --> 01:07:19.058
conclusions to impose on a particular
student. The question is how each student

01:07:19.059 --> 01:07:24.234
connects. Connection doesn’t need to look
one way. Our expectations and the context

01:07:24.235 --> 01:07:29.031
shape the interaction too, so we have
something useful to examine and change.

01:07:29.032 --> 01:07:34.017
We can make room for different ways of
being together while continuing to listen

01:07:34.018 --> 01:07:36.225
to what each person actually wants.

01:07:37.761 --> 01:07:43.325
Let’s carry that idea into the many ways
students connect. As we watch this next

01:07:43.326 --> 01:07:48.539
clip, keep the question open: how does
each student connect? Notice people,

01:07:48.540 --> 01:07:53.963
places, activities and ways of joining. A
different style of connection is not

01:07:53.964 --> 01:07:56.428
evidence that connection is absent.

01:08:41.521 --> 01:08:45.736
Keep those possibilities in mind as we
widen the picture beyond a single

01:08:45.737 --> 01:08:50.367
interaction. We can look for connection in
a student’s preferred activities and

01:08:50.368 --> 01:08:54.522
relationships, while allowing their own
ways of showing it to guide us.

01:08:56.041 --> 01:09:00.323
A student’s social world can include
family, school, community and online

01:09:00.324 --> 01:09:04.844
spaces. Connection might be one to one,
playing or working alongside someone,

01:09:04.845 --> 01:09:09.306
sharing a great deal about an interest, or
seeing someone only occasionally.

01:09:09.307 --> 01:09:14.006
Being together quietly can matter as much
as a lively conversation. Culture also

01:09:14.007 --> 01:09:18.586
shapes expectations about interaction and
relationships. We need to be careful

01:09:18.587 --> 01:09:22.988
about treating the pattern most familiar
to us as the measure of meaningful

01:09:22.989 --> 01:09:27.450
connection for everyone. There may be
spaces outside home and school where a

01:09:27.451 --> 01:09:31.912
student feels a particular sense of
belonging: a club, a community activity,

01:09:31.913 --> 01:09:36.552
or another shared-interest space. There
may be online connections too. We don’t

01:09:36.553 --> 01:09:40.478
need to disclose a student’s online
activity, identity or community

01:09:40.479 --> 01:09:44.702
membership to recognise that these
possibilities exist. Keep the picture

01:09:44.703 --> 01:09:49.223
individual. What do we know about this
student’s preferred people, places and

01:09:49.224 --> 01:09:53.625
ways of joining? What are we assuming?
What could we find out respectfully?

01:09:55.160 --> 01:10:00.290
Find a partner for a conversation about
connection. The questions are on page

01:10:00.291 --> 01:10:05.488
twelve of your Participant Guide. First,
when do the students you have in mind

01:10:05.489 --> 01:10:10.619
seem most comfortable connecting with
others at school? Second, what kinds of

01:10:10.620 --> 01:10:15.952
connection does each enjoy? That might be
talking, gaming, sport, making things,

01:10:15.953 --> 01:10:20.745
shared interests, or being together
quietly. Third, are there times when

01:10:20.746 --> 01:10:25.876
being social seems enjoyable, and times
when it seems tiring or overwhelming?

01:10:25.877 --> 01:10:31.007
Finally, what could educators change to
make connection feel safer, easier or

01:10:31.008 --> 01:10:36.205
more meaningful? Use about four minutes.
Keep identifying details private, and

01:10:36.206 --> 01:10:41.201
hold anything you don’t know as a
question. You can reflect individually if

01:10:41.202 --> 01:10:45.453
you’re watching on your own. Pause the
video for the discussion.

01:10:53.481 --> 01:10:58.319
Let’s come back to the last question: what
could change around the student?

01:10:58.320 --> 01:11:03.355
Which ideas could you use within your
role, and which would you suggest to the

01:11:03.356 --> 01:11:08.391
teacher? If you’re together, you might
offer one practical possibility without

01:11:08.392 --> 01:11:13.230
describing the student. Notice whether
your idea makes room for the kind of

01:11:13.231 --> 01:11:18.135
connection the student values. Does it
change an expectation, an activity, a

01:11:18.136 --> 01:11:23.432
space or the pace of interaction? Does it
leave room for choosing when to join and

01:11:23.433 --> 01:11:28.729
when to step back? The aim is meaningful
connection for that person. We don’t need

01:11:28.730 --> 01:11:31.607
to make everyone look social in the same
way.

01:11:33.121 --> 01:11:38.758
Now we’ll look at daily capacity.
Communication and connection are part of

01:11:38.759 --> 01:11:44.010
a much larger day, and that day asks
different amounts of energy from

01:11:44.011 --> 01:11:49.726
different students. As we move into the
next activities, we’ll use a simple

01:11:49.727 --> 01:11:55.905
question: what costs, and what lightens?
That language will help us look beyond a

01:11:55.906 --> 01:12:01.929
single task to the pattern of demands and
opportunities for recovery across the

01:12:01.930 --> 01:12:02.237
day.

01:12:03.761 --> 01:12:08.557
Think of a student’s available energy as a
battery. Each day starts with some

01:12:08.558 --> 01:12:13.354
charge, and it isn’t necessarily the same
amount as yesterday. Activities and

01:12:13.355 --> 01:12:17.898
demands draw on that energy. As the
battery runs lower, managing the next

01:12:17.899 --> 01:12:22.632
demand can become harder. You may also
hear people in the Autistic community

01:12:22.633 --> 01:12:27.618
talk about spoons. The idea is having a
limited supply available: an action uses

01:12:27.619 --> 01:12:32.479
one spoon or several, and when spoons are
low, there is less left for the next

01:12:32.480 --> 01:12:37.275
thing. These are ways of talking about
capacity, rather than a measure we can

01:12:37.276 --> 01:12:42.009
calculate for someone else. The other part
of the picture is recharging. The

01:12:42.010 --> 01:12:46.996
right supports can reduce demand and help
restore some capacity. A pause, useful

01:12:46.997 --> 01:12:51.540
information, a preferred way to
communicate or a safe connection may make

01:12:51.541 --> 01:12:56.463
a difference, depending on the person. So
we’ll look for both sides in a school

01:12:56.464 --> 01:13:01.008
day: what draws on capacity, and what
helps to restore it. What helps one

01:13:01.009 --> 01:13:03.341
student may be different for another.

01:13:04.881 --> 01:13:09.869
Capacity fluctuates. Like all of us,
Autistic students can have easier days

01:13:09.870 --> 01:13:15.061
and harder days. Stress, energy, sensory
demands and what else is happening in

01:13:15.062 --> 01:13:20.387
life can change what is manageable. That
means the same student may do something

01:13:20.388 --> 01:13:25.781
one day and need more support with it the
next. Yesterday’s success doesn’t prove

01:13:25.782 --> 01:13:31.243
that today’s difficulty is a choice, and
finishing a task doesn’t tell us how much

01:13:31.244 --> 01:13:36.367
effort it required. Hold that in mind when
you look at the students you know.

01:13:36.368 --> 01:13:41.558
We can ask what is different today, what
demands have already accumulated, and

01:13:41.559 --> 01:13:46.750
what support is available now. We don’t
have to know every cause before making

01:13:46.751 --> 01:13:52.077
information clearer or allowing a useful
pause. We do need to keep checking with

01:13:52.078 --> 01:13:57.402
the student and the people who know them,
instead of treating capacity as fixed.

01:13:58.920 --> 01:14:03.847
Turn to page thirteen. Follow the day of a
student you support from arrival to

01:14:03.847 --> 01:14:08.902
departure. Draw a simple timeline, or use
the table in your guide. Include every

01:14:08.902 --> 01:14:13.829
room and space they move through, not just
their classroom. In primary school,

01:14:13.829 --> 01:14:18.883
that might include a classroom, a library
lesson, recess and a specialist lesson

01:14:18.883 --> 01:14:24.066
after lunch. In secondary school, it may
involve a different teacher and room each

01:14:24.066 --> 01:14:29.057
period, lockers, busy corridors and the
yard. Use what belongs to your setting.

01:14:29.057 --> 01:14:33.280
Mark routines and transitions with R,
changes with C, communication

01:14:33.280 --> 01:14:37.951
opportunities with T, and moments to
choose, pause or recover with P. Then

01:14:37.951 --> 01:14:42.942
consider how the same moments affect each
of the students you have in mind. Map

01:14:42.942 --> 01:14:47.805
what you actually see. If you support a
student for only part of the day, add

01:14:47.805 --> 01:14:52.604
questions for the parts you don't know.
Leave room for costs and lighteners;

01:14:52.604 --> 01:14:57.147
we'll add those next. Keep the map
private, without names or identifying

01:14:57.147 --> 01:15:02.137
incidents. You have about six minutes,
with optional discussion with a partner.

01:15:02.137 --> 01:15:04.120
Pause the video while you work.

01:15:12.161 --> 01:15:17.067
Let’s pause our drawing there. You don’t
need a polished or complete map. This is

01:15:17.068 --> 01:15:21.790
a working picture to extend, rather than a
judgement of a student or a school.

01:15:21.791 --> 01:15:26.452
Look briefly at the same point in the day
for your different students. It may

01:15:26.453 --> 01:15:31.421
involve different communication, sensory
or task demands for each of them. That is

01:15:31.422 --> 01:15:36.328
why we’re keeping several real students in
view. Keep the map beside you; it will

01:15:36.329 --> 01:15:38.351
travel with us into Session Four.

01:15:39.880 --> 01:15:45.619
Now turn to page fourteen and return to
your day map. Use one mark or colour for

01:15:45.620 --> 01:15:50.269
what costs: what adds demand or
uncertainty. Use another for what

01:15:50.270 --> 01:15:56.082
lightens: what reduces demand, restores
capacity or brings joy. Think back to the

01:15:56.083 --> 01:16:01.386
battery image: what drains, and what might
recharge? Add questions where a

01:16:01.387 --> 01:16:05.891
student’s experience isn’t known.
Something can be enjoyable or

01:16:05.892 --> 01:16:11.413
successfully completed and still take
considerable effort. We can record what

01:16:11.414 --> 01:16:16.936
we observe, but we can’t read an internal
state from a single moment. Include

01:16:16.937 --> 01:16:22.095
strengths, joy, safe connection and
belonging. We want a picture of what

01:16:22.096 --> 01:16:27.326
sustains a student as well as what is
difficult. Give yourself about four

01:16:27.327 --> 01:16:31.613
minutes to add this layer, and pause the
video while you do.

01:16:39.641 --> 01:16:44.241
Come back to the pattern across the day.
Where do several demands occur close

01:16:44.242 --> 01:16:48.841
together? Where are the opportunities for
relief or recovery? What is already

01:16:48.842 --> 01:16:53.745
working well and worth protecting? Treat
these marks as prompts for inquiry, not a

01:16:53.746 --> 01:16:58.224
score. What costs or lightens can differ
between students, and for the same

01:16:58.225 --> 01:17:02.643
student on different days. Choose one
pattern you would like to understand

01:17:02.644 --> 01:17:07.363
better. That will help when we look more
closely at the moves between settings.

01:17:08.881 --> 01:17:13.838
This school-day stack gives us another way
to see accumulation. The darker bars

01:17:13.839 --> 01:17:18.541
represent demand. The lighter bars
represent relief and recovery. They’re a

01:17:18.542 --> 01:17:23.689
visual cue, rather than quantities we can
assign to a student. A moment that looks

01:17:23.690 --> 01:17:28.647
small by itself may arrive after many
other demands. The last transition of the

01:17:28.648 --> 01:17:32.969
day sits within everything that came
before it. Equally, a supportive

01:17:32.970 --> 01:17:37.927
interaction or an opportunity to recover
belongs in the picture too. So when we

01:17:37.928 --> 01:17:42.694
notice a difficult moment, we can ask what
the day has already asked of this

01:17:42.695 --> 01:17:47.270
student and what support has been
available. Keep this qualitative. We’re

01:17:47.271 --> 01:17:52.101
looking for patterns and questions that
help us improve access, while keeping

01:17:52.102 --> 01:17:55.087
relief, recovery and joy visible alongside
cost.

01:17:56.601 --> 01:18:01.090
There are other things that can draw on
our batteries or spoons, including effort

01:18:01.091 --> 01:18:05.524
that isn’t obvious to someone watching.
Let’s hear the next video about masking.

01:18:05.525 --> 01:18:09.957
Keep in mind the difference between how a
person appears on the outside and what

01:18:09.958 --> 01:18:11.135
they may be managing.

01:19:41.561 --> 01:19:47.148
As we return from the video, keep that
distinction open. Visible behaviour gives

01:19:47.149 --> 01:19:52.312
us part of the picture. We’ll now connect
this with identity, capacity and

01:19:52.313 --> 01:19:53.727
belonging at school.

01:19:55.241 --> 01:20:00.471
A student can appear fine without feeling
fine. Quietness, compliance or completed

01:20:00.472 --> 01:20:05.509
work doesn’t give us a full account of
what someone is managing. Masking brings

01:20:05.510 --> 01:20:10.029
that question into our thinking about
identity and belonging. Hold four

01:20:10.030 --> 01:20:14.550
questions in mind. What might this student
be working hard to manage or

01:20:14.551 --> 01:20:19.652
hide? Where can they be recognised and
included? What supports belonging without

01:20:19.653 --> 01:20:24.754
requiring them to appear less Autistic?
And what does the student want others to

01:20:24.755 --> 01:20:29.275
understand? We can’t answer those
questions simply by watching from the

01:20:29.276 --> 01:20:33.989
outside. They invite us to listen and to
examine the conditions around the

01:20:33.990 --> 01:20:38.833
student. Include what feels safe and
joyful, the connections they value, and

01:20:38.834 --> 01:20:44.129
places where they can be themselves. This
is a reason to look beyond whether a task

01:20:44.130 --> 01:20:48.714
was finished or a lesson was quiet. Access
matters, and so do the effort

01:20:48.715 --> 01:20:51.942
involved and the student’s experience of
belonging.

01:20:53.481 --> 01:20:58.349
Return to page twelve for the masking
discussion. With a partner, consider

01:20:58.350 --> 01:21:03.618
three questions. When might one of the
students you have in mind be working hard

01:21:03.619 --> 01:21:08.620
to appear fine at school when they are
not? When and where do they seem most

01:21:08.621 --> 01:21:13.422
comfortable being themselves? What could
we change to help them feel more

01:21:13.423 --> 01:21:18.757
comfortable and supported? You have about
three minutes. Keep examples anonymised

01:21:18.758 --> 01:21:23.960
and distinguish what you know from what
you wonder. Avoid assuming that a quiet

01:21:23.961 --> 01:21:28.628
student is coping, or that outward
behaviour tells you the whole story.

01:21:28.629 --> 01:21:31.096
Pause the video for the conversation.

01:21:39.121 --> 01:21:44.176
Let’s come back together. Focus on one
possible change, rather than a conclusion

01:21:44.177 --> 01:21:49.359
about what a student must be feeling. If
you’re sharing with colleagues, offer the

01:21:49.360 --> 01:21:54.479
change without identifying the student.
Perhaps your next step is to ask a better

01:21:54.480 --> 01:21:59.214
question, understand an existing source of
comfort, or change a demand in a

01:21:59.215 --> 01:22:04.398
routine. Which possibility is within your
role, and which would you raise with the

01:22:04.399 --> 01:22:09.005
teacher? Seeing a student relax in one
space and work hard to hold things

01:22:09.006 --> 01:22:13.613
together in another can be useful to
share. Whatever you choose, keep the

01:22:13.614 --> 01:22:18.860
student’s perspective central. The aim is
to make being themselves safer and better

01:22:18.861 --> 01:22:22.122
supported, and to find out whether the
change helps.

01:22:23.641 --> 01:22:28.689
You often see a student's day closely,
across different rooms and from one hour

01:22:28.690 --> 01:22:33.544
to the next. You may notice a change
before others do: going quiet, pressing

01:22:33.545 --> 01:22:38.722
harder on a pencil or stiffening. Those
are useful observations. They do not tell

01:22:38.723 --> 01:22:43.641
us the reason on their own. The four
points here are early signs, what rather

01:22:43.642 --> 01:22:48.625
than why, keep it short, and pass it on.
Write what you saw and heard, when it

01:22:48.626 --> 01:22:53.156
happened, what came before and what
helped. Keep guesses about feelings

01:22:53.157 --> 01:22:58.270
separate from the observation. Here's the
example from the guide: 'Period three,

01:22:58.271 --> 01:23:03.448
after the move from the science room. Put
head down and stopped writing at eleven

01:23:03.449 --> 01:23:08.497
twenty. Back to work after five minutes at
the side desk with headphones.' That

01:23:08.498 --> 01:23:13.287
gives the teacher something to work with.
'Was anxious all morning' gives a

01:23:13.288 --> 01:23:17.753
conclusion without the observations behind
it. Now think of one recent

01:23:17.754 --> 01:23:22.414
moment, or use a made-up example. Write a
two-line note on paper, without

01:23:22.415 --> 01:23:27.398
identifying details. Allow two minutes to
write and a minute to compare with a

01:23:27.399 --> 01:23:32.512
partner. Is it what you saw and heard? Is
there a guess you could remove or turn

01:23:32.513 --> 01:23:37.625
into a question? Then practise asking, in
your own words, 'What should I know to

01:23:37.626 --> 01:23:42.027
support them well?' That is about
information you need for your role,

01:23:42.028 --> 01:23:46.687
rather than seeking a student's
confidential history. Pause here to write

01:23:46.688 --> 01:23:47.463
and compare.

01:23:55.481 --> 01:24:00.166
Let's come back. What makes your note
useful to the teacher? A time, a context,

01:24:00.167 --> 01:24:04.853
an observation and something that helped
can be more useful than a long account

01:24:04.854 --> 01:24:08.998
full of interpretations. Use your school's
agreed way of recording and

01:24:08.999 --> 01:24:13.865
passing on information. The teacher plans
adjustments; your observations help them

01:24:13.866 --> 01:24:18.611
plan and review. If you regularly lack the
information you need to do your role,

01:24:18.612 --> 01:24:23.058
raise that through the school's usual
pathway. You can ask what you need to

01:24:23.059 --> 01:24:26.361
know without needing every detail of the
student's life.

01:24:27.881 --> 01:24:33.511
When a student becomes overwhelmed, you
may be the closest adult. Everything we

01:24:33.512 --> 01:24:39.070
discuss here sits within the student's
agreed plan and your school's policies.

01:24:39.071 --> 01:24:43.906
This is support, not physical
intervention. The first point is fewer

01:24:43.907 --> 01:24:49.754
words. Speak quietly and reduce how much
information the student needs to process.

01:24:49.755 --> 01:24:55.312
The second is space and dignity. Reduce
the audience and avoid crowding, while

01:24:55.313 --> 01:25:00.943
staying available. Third, offer rather
than demand: a known way out, water or a

01:25:00.944 --> 01:25:06.646
familiar calming tool may be offered as
choices, where that fits the student and

01:25:06.647 --> 01:25:12.349
their plan. Afterwards, recovery comes
first. Discussion about what happened can

01:25:12.350 --> 01:25:17.764
wait. Then record what preceded the
situation and pass it on. Let's practise

01:25:17.765 --> 01:25:23.394
the wording without acting out distress.
Here is a long adult response from the

01:25:23.395 --> 01:25:28.953
guide: 'What's going on? You need to take
some deep breaths and tell me what's

01:25:28.954 --> 01:25:34.656
wrong, because I can't help you unless you
talk to me.' How could you reduce the

01:25:34.657 --> 01:25:40.287
words and make room for a choice? Write a
shorter version. Then think privately

01:25:40.288 --> 01:25:45.629
about your actual role: what does the
student's plan ask you to do? What is

01:25:45.630 --> 01:25:51.332
unclear, and who should you contact? If
something is unclear, that is a question

01:25:51.333 --> 01:25:56.530
for the appropriate person in your school.
Pause the video for this brief

01:25:56.531 --> 01:25:57.322
reflection.

01:26:05.361 --> 01:26:10.292
Let's return. The guide offers examples
such as, 'I'm here. Water?' or, 'Quiet

01:26:10.293 --> 01:26:15.288
room or stay here?' The difference is less
to process and a choice, rather than

01:26:15.289 --> 01:26:20.220
a demand for an explanation. These are
illustrations to consider, not a script

01:26:20.221 --> 01:26:25.217
or sequence that suits every student. Keep
the person's known communication and

01:26:25.218 --> 01:26:30.085
support needs central. Stay available
without crowding or standing over them.

01:26:30.086 --> 01:26:34.889
Follow their plan and your school's
policy, and use the school's process for

01:26:34.890 --> 01:26:39.308
any safety or wellbeing concern.
Afterwards, the short factual note we

01:26:39.309 --> 01:26:43.664
just practised can help the team
understand what came before and what

01:26:43.665 --> 01:26:44.880
supported recovery.

01:26:46.401 --> 01:26:51.720
Turn to the audit across settings on page
fifteen, with your day map beside you.

01:26:51.721 --> 01:26:57.039
Follow one student through the classroom,
a specialist lesson, the playground or

01:26:57.040 --> 01:27:01.955
yard, the library or canteen, and the
corridors and moves in between. Look

01:27:01.956 --> 01:27:07.140
closely at the thresholds: the point where
a student moves from one setting to

01:27:07.141 --> 01:27:12.392
another. What changes in sound, people,
instructions, expectations or available

01:27:12.393 --> 01:27:17.510
support? Does their visual schedule or
communication system travel with them?

01:27:17.511 --> 01:27:22.829
Use the four steps. Name the setting or
move. Write what can be observed without

01:27:22.830 --> 01:27:27.139
interpretation. List possible
environmental or task contributors.

01:27:27.140 --> 01:27:32.257
Record what lightens demand and one
question to investigate. For example, you

01:27:32.258 --> 01:27:37.509
might examine a primary student's walk to
the library, or a secondary student's

01:27:37.510 --> 01:27:42.155
change of room at the bell. Use your
actual context. You don't need to

01:27:42.156 --> 01:27:46.936
complete every setting: two or three, with
a clear move between them, is

01:27:46.937 --> 01:27:52.054
enough. Record questions for places you
don't see. Keep names and identifying

01:27:52.055 --> 01:27:57.575
details out. Choose one observation worth
sharing with the teacher. Allow about six

01:27:57.576 --> 01:28:00.470
minutes, and pause the video while you
work.

01:28:08.481 --> 01:28:13.051
Look across your audit. A difference in
participation may tell us something about

01:28:13.052 --> 01:28:17.051
the setting as well as the student. Have
you separated observation from

01:28:17.052 --> 01:28:21.280
interpretation? Have you included the
support that already helps? Your view

01:28:21.281 --> 01:28:25.394
across different places is useful,
particularly at handovers. Consistency

01:28:25.395 --> 01:28:29.851
depends on the team sharing what they
know. Choose one setting or move to carry

01:28:29.852 --> 01:28:34.422
into design, and keep the question open
where the student's experience is not yet

01:28:34.423 --> 01:28:34.763
known.

01:28:36.281 --> 01:28:41.457
On page sixteen, or your Carry into design
card, choose a clear focus for the

01:28:41.458 --> 01:28:46.634
next session. There are three parts: Keep,
Reduce and Explore. For Keep, name

01:28:46.635 --> 01:28:51.743
one support worth protecting because it
helps access, connection or joy. For

01:28:51.744 --> 01:28:57.124
Reduce, name one barrier or unnecessary
demand. Tick where it belongs: in class,

01:28:57.125 --> 01:29:02.165
between settings, or breaks and play. We
will use those three categories to

01:29:02.166 --> 01:29:07.274
regroup after lunch. An unnecessary demand
might be copying a long task from

01:29:07.275 --> 01:29:12.724
the board when a printed copy would serve
the same purpose. That is an example to

01:29:12.725 --> 01:29:18.037
consider, not a conclusion about your
student's task. Keep the idea within what

01:29:18.038 --> 01:29:23.214
you can do or suggest to the teacher. For
Explore, write one question for the

01:29:23.215 --> 01:29:28.255
student, the teacher or the family. Allow
about three minutes, keeping your

01:29:28.256 --> 01:29:33.500
responses private if you wish. Pause now
to finish the card, then keep it with

01:29:33.501 --> 01:29:34.793
your map and audit.

01:29:42.801 --> 01:29:47.596
Keep students first as you look at those
choices. Protect what supports access,

01:29:47.597 --> 01:29:51.592
connection and joy, as well as identifying
a barrier to reduce. An

01:29:51.593 --> 01:29:56.449
unanswered question can guide a useful
conversation. After lunch, we'll focus on

01:29:56.450 --> 01:30:01.490
the support you give and how it can build
independence, within what the teacher has

01:30:01.491 --> 01:30:05.855
planned. The live workshop lunch runs from
twelve thirty to one. In this

01:30:05.856 --> 01:30:10.589
recording, take a break between sessions
when you need it. Before we close the

01:30:10.590 --> 01:30:15.260
morning, let's return to the fuller
picture of the students you have in mind.

01:30:16.801 --> 01:30:21.135
Across Sessions Two and Three, you’ve been
building a picture of those

01:30:21.136 --> 01:30:25.972
students. How the world arrives through
their senses. How each thinks, focuses

01:30:25.973 --> 01:30:31.123
and builds understanding. Their strengths
and interests. Their internal experience.

01:30:31.124 --> 01:30:36.023
How they reach towards other people, and
what that can cost when the conditions

01:30:36.024 --> 01:30:40.798
don’t support them. Managing a school day
can involve sensory demands, social

01:30:40.799 --> 01:30:45.132
interpretation, executive effort and
communication across differences.

01:30:45.133 --> 01:30:49.718
Sometimes it also involves working to
appear fine when fine is not what is

01:30:49.719 --> 01:30:54.807
happening. That effort uses resources. A
student who knows who they are, and feels

01:30:54.808 --> 01:30:58.890
valued for it, has more of those resources
available for learning,

01:30:58.891 --> 01:31:03.225
connection and self-knowledge. The
research discussed in this workshop

01:31:03.226 --> 01:31:07.936
connects a positive sense of Autistic
identity with better mental health and

01:31:07.937 --> 01:31:12.648
wellbeing. Belonging matters. So does
agency: meaningful choice about how to

01:31:12.649 --> 01:31:17.360
communicate, where to be, and when to step
back. Knowing that other Autistic

01:31:17.361 --> 01:31:21.945
people share your experience can matter
too. There are Autistic adults and

01:31:21.946 --> 01:31:26.531
communities in which people find belonging
and purpose. These students are

01:31:26.532 --> 01:31:31.055
not alone. This belongs within our
educational work. We can make room for

01:31:31.056 --> 01:31:35.012
identity, connection and
self-understanding in the conditions we

01:31:35.013 --> 01:31:39.849
create every day. Take that thought with
you into the break. You can help them

01:31:39.850 --> 01:31:43.178
know themselves, and know that who they
are is valued.

01:31:53.201 --> 01:31:58.208
Welcome back. This session is about
support that builds independence. Have

01:31:58.209 --> 01:32:03.696
your Carry into design card, school-day
map and audit across settings nearby. The

01:32:03.697 --> 01:32:09.185
session summary is on page seventeen of
your Participant Guide. We'll work on the

01:32:09.186 --> 01:32:14.536
part of the day you shape most directly:
how support is given. Each person will

01:32:14.537 --> 01:32:19.612
choose one support moment and plan a small
step towards the student doing a

01:32:19.613 --> 01:32:24.964
little more themselves. You will discuss
that step with the teacher and include

01:32:24.965 --> 01:32:30.040
the student's view before changing
support. Keep the whole student in view.

01:32:30.041 --> 01:32:35.460
What you know about them is not a list of
deficits. It is the starting point for

01:32:35.461 --> 01:32:39.919
understanding the environment, task,
routine and support that make

01:32:39.920 --> 01:32:41.497
participation possible.

01:32:43.001 --> 01:32:47.508
Students come first. Before choosing a
strategy, return to the real students

01:32:47.509 --> 01:32:51.776
you've been thinking about and the
specific access questions on your day

01:32:51.777 --> 01:32:56.285
map. Each of you will design one change to
a support you give, then check it

01:32:56.286 --> 01:33:00.733
against the two or three different
students in mind. The same idea may help

01:33:00.734 --> 01:33:05.301
one person and do little for another. That
matters. Keep observation separate

01:33:05.302 --> 01:33:09.689
from interpretation. If you don't know why
something happens, hold it as a

01:33:09.690 --> 01:33:14.017
question for the student, teacher or
family. A useful brief connects what

01:33:14.018 --> 01:33:18.766
matters for the student with something
that could change in the support, task or

01:33:18.767 --> 01:33:23.274
routine. We are not diagnosing, ranking
students or reducing them to support

01:33:23.275 --> 01:33:23.633
needs.

01:33:25.161 --> 01:33:30.077
Our central model is the independence
ladder: scaffold, don't substitute. Let's

01:33:30.078 --> 01:33:34.867
walk through the five rungs. Doing for
means the adult does the task. Helping

01:33:34.868 --> 01:33:39.910
means the adult does part of it with the
student. Prompting means the adult gives

01:33:39.911 --> 01:33:44.385
a cue and the student does it. Coaching
means the adult checks in from a

01:33:44.386 --> 01:33:49.050
distance. On their own means the student
does it without the adult doing or

01:33:49.051 --> 01:33:53.336
prompting that step. Take the writing-task
example from the guide. At

01:33:53.337 --> 01:33:58.315
first, an adult gets the book out, finds
the page and writes the date. Next, the

01:33:58.316 --> 01:34:03.106
adult finds the page and the student
writes the date. A further step might be

01:34:03.107 --> 01:34:07.644
pointing to the book and the first step on
the board. Coaching could mean

01:34:07.645 --> 01:34:12.498
checking in from across the room once the
student has started. Eventually, the

01:34:12.499 --> 01:34:17.414
student may use a first-step card without
the adult beside them. In a secondary

01:34:17.415 --> 01:34:22.394
computer lesson, the same idea could move
from opening the file for a student to

01:34:22.395 --> 01:34:27.374
the student following accessible written
steps. A student can be doing something

01:34:27.375 --> 01:34:32.038
independently while still using a useful
tool. Many support staff have been

01:34:32.039 --> 01:34:36.829
thanked for being the person a student
relies on. There is care in that work.

01:34:36.830 --> 01:34:41.682
Here we are asking how that care can help
the student need a little less adult

01:34:41.683 --> 01:34:45.905
support over time. The ladder is a
planning prompt, not an automatic

01:34:45.906 --> 01:34:50.381
timetable for removing help. Different
tasks, settings and days can need

01:34:50.382 --> 01:34:55.234
different levels. Needed support can stay,
change or return. Fading is planned

01:34:55.235 --> 01:35:00.214
with the teacher and student. Think of one
support you gave this week. Where did

01:35:00.215 --> 01:35:05.194
it sit on this ladder? Pause for a brief
reflection, or an optional example with

01:35:05.195 --> 01:35:05.887
colleagues.

01:35:13.921 --> 01:35:18.606
Keep that moment in mind. This isn't a
judgement of how much support you gave.

01:35:18.607 --> 01:35:23.413
The useful question is what that moment
required, what the student could already

01:35:23.414 --> 01:35:28.160
do, and whether there is one manageable
next step to plan together. It can take

01:35:28.161 --> 01:35:30.777
more thought to prompt less than to do
more.

01:35:32.281 --> 01:35:37.400
Here is the rhythm of the session: short
inputs, then doing. We'll start by

01:35:37.401 --> 01:35:42.589
regrouping, write a brief, consider four
options and check access. Then each

01:35:42.590 --> 01:35:47.501
person designs one step up the ladder,
checks it privately against their

01:35:47.502 --> 01:35:52.482
students, and considers where further
support is needed. We finish with a

01:35:52.483 --> 01:35:57.948
gallery and a commitment. You each keep
your own support moment. Your trio helps

01:35:57.949 --> 01:36:03.068
you sharpen the thinking. The aim is one
realistic step to discuss, try and

01:36:03.069 --> 01:36:06.180
review, rather than a long list of
strategies.

01:36:07.721 --> 01:36:13.009
Bring back your Carry into design card
from Session Three. Look at your Reduce

01:36:13.010 --> 01:36:18.298
item. Is the support you want to work on
mainly in class, between settings, or

01:36:18.299 --> 01:36:23.450
during breaks and play? In the live
workshop, those are three corners of the

01:36:23.451 --> 01:36:28.533
room. Move to the area that fits your
focus and form a trio with two people

01:36:28.534 --> 01:36:33.822
nearby. Choose the support you most want
to examine if your role spans several

01:36:33.823 --> 01:36:38.836
settings. Join a nearby pair if your
corner is small. Each person needs an

01:36:38.837 --> 01:36:44.469
Independence ladder sheet. Its first page
holds your brief and plan. The second has

01:36:44.470 --> 01:36:49.483
the private My students cards. You can
also use the matching pages in your

01:36:49.484 --> 01:36:54.635
Participant Guide. If you're watching
independently, make the same choice on

01:36:54.636 --> 01:36:59.855
paper and arrange a colleague check later.
Allow a few minutes to regroup and

01:36:59.856 --> 01:37:04.938
have the sheet ready. Pause the video now,
then resume with your own two or

01:37:04.939 --> 01:37:06.930
three students still in mind.

01:37:14.961 --> 01:37:19.268
Keep your own support moment in front of
you, even if other people in the trio

01:37:19.269 --> 01:37:23.800
have chosen something different. You are
helping one another think; you don't need

01:37:23.801 --> 01:37:26.709
one shared plan for three different sets
of students.

01:37:28.241 --> 01:37:33.678
Hold a concise picture of each student as
you design: strengths, interests and joy;

01:37:33.679 --> 01:37:38.719
communication, sensory experience and
thinking; what costs and what lightens;

01:37:38.720 --> 01:37:43.892
identity and belonging. Include what the
student, family and team already know,

01:37:43.893 --> 01:37:49.330
and the questions still open. You may see
the student across many rooms and spaces.

01:37:49.331 --> 01:37:54.569
That view is useful here. Use what you
already know to do your role. This is not

01:37:54.570 --> 01:37:59.411
a request to gather confidential
information or build a file on a student.

01:37:59.412 --> 01:38:04.584
Later you will check your plan against
each of these different people. For now,

01:38:04.585 --> 01:38:08.363
keep their strengths and experiences
alongside your brief.

01:38:09.881 --> 01:38:14.940
Choose one support moment you give. Use
section one of the Independence ladder

01:38:14.941 --> 01:38:19.934
sheet, or My brief on page eighteen. Tick
where it happens: in class, between

01:38:19.935 --> 01:38:25.125
settings, or breaks and play. Complete
three stems. At the moment, I support by.

01:38:25.126 --> 01:38:30.316
The student can already. One step more
independence would look like. Here is the

01:38:30.317 --> 01:38:35.507
example from the guide. 'At the moment, I
walk beside the student to the library

01:38:35.508 --> 01:38:40.501
and tell them what happens next. The
student can already find the library and

01:38:40.502 --> 01:38:45.560
follow the class line. One step more
independence could be walking with a peer

01:38:45.561 --> 01:38:50.686
while I follow a little behind, with the
change shown on their visual timetable

01:38:50.687 --> 01:38:55.746
first.' That is a proposal to discuss, not
a change to make without agreement.

01:38:55.747 --> 01:39:00.937
Keep your brief that specific. Name what
the student can already do, rather than

01:39:00.938 --> 01:39:06.128
only what is difficult. Check for blame or
deficit labels, and keep questions as

01:39:06.129 --> 01:39:11.450
questions. Allow four minutes on your own
and two minutes to share your brief with

01:39:11.451 --> 01:39:16.313
the trio and take one suggestion. Use
initials or roles only. Pause here to

01:39:16.314 --> 01:39:16.707
write.

01:39:24.721 --> 01:39:29.931
Let's return to your three lines. Is the
step small enough to picture? Does it

01:39:29.932 --> 01:39:34.737
build from something the student can
already do? If it describes several

01:39:34.738 --> 01:39:39.811
changes, choose one. Keep this same brief
active as we consider the options.

01:39:41.321 --> 01:39:46.449
There are four short inputs: five moves
that belong to you, predictability,

01:39:46.450 --> 01:39:51.579
designed distance, and visual supports and
social stories. Listen with your

01:39:51.580 --> 01:39:56.847
brief in front of you. Which might help
the particular step you've named? The

01:39:56.848 --> 01:40:02.322
workshop notes refer to printed option
cards, but separate cards aren't included

01:40:02.323 --> 01:40:07.452
in these assets. The headings are on
screen and on your Independence ladder

01:40:07.453 --> 01:40:12.928
sheet, so you can use those to make your
choice. We'll choose one or two options

01:40:12.929 --> 01:40:14.106
after the inputs.

01:40:15.641 --> 01:40:20.437
These are small moves you can use
alongside the teacher's plan and the

01:40:20.438 --> 01:40:26.067
student's agreed supports. The first is a
two-minute warning before a change. 'Two

01:40:26.068 --> 01:40:31.211
minutes, then we pack up' may be shown on
a timer or card if that makes the

01:40:31.212 --> 01:40:36.564
information accessible. The second is
fewer words when stress appears. Shorten

01:40:36.565 --> 01:40:41.430
the sentence, offer one piece of
information at a time and wait, rather

01:40:41.431 --> 01:40:46.226
than filling the space with more
instructions. The third is making the

01:40:46.227 --> 01:40:51.718
first step visible. That could be a short
written step on the desk, or the first

01:40:51.719 --> 01:40:56.793
question highlighted on a page. The useful
part is information the student

01:40:56.794 --> 01:41:02.145
can return to, instead of needing an adult
to repeat it. The fourth is a short

01:41:02.146 --> 01:41:07.567
observation note: what you saw, when, what
came before and what helped. Pass it

01:41:07.568 --> 01:41:12.433
to the teacher through the school's agreed
process, keeping observation

01:41:12.434 --> 01:41:17.925
separate from interpretation. Our fifth
move is designed distance, which has its

01:41:17.926 --> 01:41:23.417
own slide shortly. All five connect to the
morning's thinking about transitions,

01:41:23.418 --> 01:41:28.839
processing and communication. A prompt
that is available in the environment can

01:41:28.840 --> 01:41:33.705
sometimes replace one delivered repeatedly
by a person. As you consider

01:41:33.706 --> 01:41:38.988
each move, notice whether it is already
part of your practice or something to

01:41:38.989 --> 01:41:44.549
discuss using next week. Keep the focus on
what fits your brief and the student's

01:41:44.550 --> 01:41:45.592
agreed support.

01:41:47.121 --> 01:41:51.487
Predictability means access to information
about routine and change. It

01:41:51.488 --> 01:41:56.478
doesn't mean making every day identical or
promising that nothing unexpected will

01:41:56.479 --> 01:42:01.219
happen. How much information in the day is
spoken, implied or held by adults?

01:42:01.220 --> 01:42:05.960
When a plan changes, does the student find
out only when you tell them? Could

01:42:05.961 --> 01:42:10.327
they return to the information themselves?
The four questions are: what

01:42:10.328 --> 01:42:15.130
is happening, what is changing, what comes
next, and what can I do if the plan

01:42:15.131 --> 01:42:19.684
changes? Think of an assembly moved
earlier, a relief teacher, or a lesson

01:42:19.685 --> 01:42:24.613
moved to a different room. How is that
change shown? Is the student's support up

01:42:24.614 --> 01:42:29.229
to date? Can they ask a question or signal
that they need more information?

01:42:29.230 --> 01:42:33.970
Give information early enough to be
useful, in a form the student can access.

01:42:33.971 --> 01:42:38.400
That can reduce reliance on an adult
explaining every change while still

01:42:38.401 --> 01:42:42.891
leaving help available. Return to your
audit across settings: where might

01:42:42.892 --> 01:42:45.448
information be lost at a move or handover?

01:42:46.961 --> 01:42:51.855
Designed distance asks us to consider
where we stand. Sometimes, without anyone

01:42:51.856 --> 01:42:56.751
choosing it, an adult becomes the person
always beside a student. Peers talk to

01:42:56.752 --> 01:43:01.521
the adult or go around them, and the
teacher passes things through the adult.

01:43:01.522 --> 01:43:06.165
The student's contact with peers and the
teacher can gradually shrink. That

01:43:06.166 --> 01:43:10.872
pattern can grow from care, and from being
asked to stay close. The question

01:43:10.873 --> 01:43:15.830
is how to make room for the student's own
first try and for other relationships,

01:43:15.831 --> 01:43:20.851
while keeping needed help available. The
guide gives examples: setting up partner

01:43:20.852 --> 01:43:25.307
reading and then checking in from the next
table; helping a pair begin a

01:43:25.308 --> 01:43:30.202
practical task and then stepping to the
side; staying within sight while a peer

01:43:30.203 --> 01:43:35.161
game gets going. These are possibilities
to consider with the teacher, not fixed

01:43:35.162 --> 01:43:39.930
positions everyone should use. Some
students need close support at particular

01:43:39.931 --> 01:43:44.888
times, including for safety, distress,
personal care or health needs. Follow the

01:43:44.889 --> 01:43:49.784
student's agreed plan and school policy.
Designed distance means planning where

01:43:49.785 --> 01:43:54.554
support is useful, rather than simply
withdrawing it. The image on this slide

01:43:54.555 --> 01:43:59.386
is still a placeholder. Use the question
on screen: is there one lesson where,

01:43:59.387 --> 01:44:04.219
with the teacher's agreement, you could
stand a little further back and notice

01:44:04.220 --> 01:44:08.926
what changes? Make a brief note, and pause
if you'd like time to discuss it.

01:44:16.961 --> 01:44:21.388
Keep that possibility alongside your
brief. Check what would keep the student

01:44:21.389 --> 01:44:25.699
supported and able to communicate a need
for help. The aim is more room for

01:44:25.700 --> 01:44:29.777
agency, peers and the teacher, with a
clear way to review what happens.

01:44:31.281 --> 01:44:36.803
A visual support should clarify something
the student needs to find, understand,

01:44:36.804 --> 01:44:42.396
remember or communicate. Start with that
purpose before choosing a tool. Objects,

01:44:42.397 --> 01:44:47.290
photographs, written words, symbols,
checklists, maps and schedules are

01:44:47.291 --> 01:44:52.254
possible formats. A support isn't
automatically accessible because it is

01:44:52.255 --> 01:44:57.288
visual. Check its meaning with the
student, keep it available where it is

01:44:57.289 --> 01:45:02.881
used and remove unnecessary detail. The
same question matters for social stories:

01:45:02.882 --> 01:45:08.405
what is this for? The guide contrasts a
story asking a student to look at people

01:45:08.406 --> 01:45:14.068
when they speak with a story helping them
understand a fire drill or an excursion.

01:45:14.069 --> 01:45:18.542
One measures how typical the student
looks. The other can support

01:45:18.543 --> 01:45:23.576
understanding, safety and access,
particularly when made with the student

01:45:23.577 --> 01:45:28.959
in their own interests and words. Many
people have created these supports with

01:45:28.960 --> 01:45:34.483
care. We're reviewing their purpose and
usefulness, rather than asking anyone to

01:45:34.484 --> 01:45:40.006
rewrite one during this activity. Changes
to a student's supports go through the

01:45:40.007 --> 01:45:44.970
teacher and school processes. The four
review questions are also on page

01:45:44.971 --> 01:45:50.213
nineteen. Does it help the student, or
help the student look typical? Was it

01:45:50.214 --> 01:45:55.876
made with the student? Does it match what
is actually happening? Could the student

01:45:55.877 --> 01:46:00.700
use it without you? Choose one support you
use and think through those

01:46:00.701 --> 01:46:04.825
questions. Keep the student's
communication system available

01:46:04.826 --> 01:46:09.929
throughout; no spoken answer does not mean
no understanding or preference.

01:46:09.930 --> 01:46:13.353
Pause briefly if you want to discuss your
example.

01:46:21.361 --> 01:46:26.298
Keep one question from that review to take
to the teacher. The Visual Supports

01:46:26.299 --> 01:46:31.172
Checklist and an online webinar are
referenced here for further learning, but

01:46:31.173 --> 01:46:36.366
their specific access details still need
to be confirmed by the workshop team. The

01:46:36.367 --> 01:46:41.175
slide's image is also a placeholder. For
your design today, keep the purpose

01:46:41.176 --> 01:46:45.280
clear: information the student can
understand and use, with their

01:46:45.281 --> 01:46:47.459
communication and agency included.

01:46:48.961 --> 01:46:53.998
Choose one or two options that answer your
brief. You don't need all four. Look

01:46:53.999 --> 01:46:59.037
again at the five moves, predictability,
designed distance, and visual supports

01:46:59.038 --> 01:47:04.075
and social stories. Something else may fit
better if it is grounded in what you

01:47:04.076 --> 01:47:09.307
know and the agreed support. Record your
choice in section two of the Independence

01:47:09.308 --> 01:47:14.281
ladder sheet, or on page eighteen. Write
why it fits. Which step up the ladder

01:47:14.282 --> 01:47:19.384
might it help? Your trio can help make
that connection more precise. Allow about

01:47:19.385 --> 01:47:23.000
two minutes. Pause here to choose and
record your reason.

01:47:31.001 --> 01:47:36.351
Read your reason back. The point is to
match support to your brief, rather than

01:47:36.352 --> 01:47:41.838
collect strategies. If you can't explain
how an option helps the specific moment,

01:47:41.839 --> 01:47:45.952
return to what the student needs to
understand, access or do.

01:47:47.481 --> 01:47:53.000
Now apply a Universal Design for Learning
lens. Make important information clear

01:47:53.001 --> 01:47:57.821
and available. Offer workable ways to
participate and respond. Support

01:47:57.822 --> 01:48:03.201
engagement while retaining meaningful
challenge. The teacher plans adjustments

01:48:03.202 --> 01:48:08.651
and you help deliver them. Your presence,
by itself, is not the adjustment. The

01:48:08.652 --> 01:48:12.982
useful change might be clearer
information, a visual support, a

01:48:12.983 --> 01:48:18.362
different way to respond or a change to
the environment. You can help put that

01:48:18.363 --> 01:48:23.952
change into practice, notice how it works
and feed back. Access should not depend

01:48:23.953 --> 01:48:29.122
on one person always being beside the
student. At the same time, individual

01:48:29.123 --> 01:48:34.363
support may remain necessary. Universal
design and knowledge of a particular

01:48:34.364 --> 01:48:39.743
student inform one another. We will use
that thinking to check the draft, then

01:48:39.744 --> 01:48:41.628
return to individual needs.

01:48:43.161 --> 01:48:48.421
Use section three of your sheet, or
Universal first on page nineteen. Your

01:48:48.422 --> 01:48:53.465
trio will help each person run the
questions over their own design. Can

01:48:53.466 --> 01:48:59.158
students find and understand what they
need? Can they take part in more than one

01:48:59.159 --> 01:49:04.491
way? Can they pause, ask for help or
recover? Is the support available when

01:49:04.492 --> 01:49:10.328
demand increases? The sheet and guide add
a fifth question: is there more than one

01:49:10.329 --> 01:49:16.165
way to access, engage and respond? Where
your answer is not yet, adjust the design

01:49:16.166 --> 01:49:21.858
and note what you changed. Ask whether the
information or support would still be

01:49:21.859 --> 01:49:27.335
available if you weren't there. What would
another staff member need to know?

01:49:27.336 --> 01:49:32.668
The presence of a resource doesn't
establish that it works. Access needs to

01:49:32.669 --> 01:49:38.288
be checked through use and feedback. For
now, allow about five minutes for your

01:49:38.289 --> 01:49:42.323
trio to help strengthen each draft. Pause
while you work.

01:49:50.361 --> 01:49:55.981
Let's return. Keep the changes you've made
visible on the sheet. A question you

01:49:55.982 --> 01:50:01.674
can't answer yet is useful information for
the teacher conversation. We're ready

01:50:01.675 --> 01:50:07.078
to turn the draft into one small step,
with observation and review built in.

01:50:08.601 --> 01:50:13.565
Use section four of the Independence
ladder sheet, or page twenty of your

01:50:13.566 --> 01:50:18.943
guide. Plan one step up the ladder for the
support moment you've chosen. First,

01:50:18.944 --> 01:50:24.046
one way to fade support, starting next
week if it is agreed and ready. Name

01:50:24.047 --> 01:50:29.010
exactly what you might do less of and what
takes its place. It could be a

01:50:29.011 --> 01:50:34.251
gesture replacing a spoken prompt, a
first-step card replacing sitting beside

01:50:34.252 --> 01:50:39.767
the student, or a peer walking with the
student while an adult follows. These are

01:50:39.768 --> 01:50:44.939
examples, not instructions to change
support on your own. Second, choose one

01:50:44.940 --> 01:50:50.386
communication tool to practise this term.
Use a tool the student already uses or

01:50:50.387 --> 01:50:55.420
that is part of their agreed supports.
Consistent use may matter more than

01:50:55.421 --> 01:51:00.660
adding another tool. Third, plan one
conversation with the teacher. Write one

01:51:00.661 --> 01:51:06.108
thing you've noticed and one question.
That conversation may need to come before

01:51:06.109 --> 01:51:10.865
any change starts. Include how the
student's view will shape the step.

01:51:10.866 --> 01:51:15.554
Fourth, decide what you will observe. Look
for access, participation,

01:51:15.555 --> 01:51:20.519
understanding and the student's
experience, rather than compliance alone.

01:51:20.520 --> 01:51:25.829
Fifth, set a time to review together. Keep
it to one step, not several. Fading

01:51:25.830 --> 01:51:31.483
isn't automatic or permanent. Support can
stay, change or return through the agreed

01:51:31.484 --> 01:51:36.930
process. Allow twelve minutes, then ask
your trio to check that the plan remains

01:51:36.931 --> 01:51:41.619
student-centred and builds independence.
Pause now to make your plan.

01:51:49.641 --> 01:51:54.570
Prepare a one-sentence description of the
step you've planned. Does it say what you

01:51:54.571 --> 01:51:59.259
will do less of and what support remains
available? Is the teacher conversation

01:51:59.260 --> 01:52:04.129
clear? Have you included the student and a
review time? Keep this as a proposal to

01:52:04.130 --> 01:52:08.277
discuss and refine. The plan connects your
close observations with the

01:52:08.278 --> 01:52:12.485
teacher's planning, rather than making you
responsible for changing the

01:52:12.486 --> 01:52:13.928
student's support alone.

01:52:15.441 --> 01:52:20.069
Now work privately with one of the My
students cards from page two of the

01:52:20.070 --> 01:52:25.149
handout, or use page twenty-one of your
guide. Keep this separate from the sheet

01:52:25.150 --> 01:52:29.971
that may go on the wall. Hold your
proposed plan against Student A, B and C.

01:52:29.972 --> 01:52:34.986
For each, consider helps, no difference,
or needs something more. Your students

01:52:34.987 --> 01:52:40.066
may be in different classes or settings,
so the same design may work differently

01:52:40.067 --> 01:52:45.209
for each. These are planning judgements to
test, not evidence that the change has

01:52:45.210 --> 01:52:50.160
already worked. Keep uncertainty visible.
Share only what you choose with your

01:52:50.161 --> 01:52:55.175
trio, without identifying students. You
might simply say where the design still

01:52:55.176 --> 01:53:00.190
needs something more. Allow six minutes.
Pause here for the private check, then

01:53:00.191 --> 01:53:03.598
return to consider what further support
could involve.

01:53:11.601 --> 01:53:16.214
Look at where you marked needs something
more, or where the effect is uncertain.

01:53:16.215 --> 01:53:20.711
That does not mean the student has failed
the design. It tells us where a more

01:53:20.712 --> 01:53:25.091
targeted or individual response may be
needed. Keep that question open as we

01:53:25.092 --> 01:53:27.018
look at the continuum of support.

01:53:28.521 --> 01:53:33.031
Support is a continuum, rather than a
single intervention. Start with an

01:53:33.032 --> 01:53:37.225
inclusive environment: predictable
routines, clear expectations and

01:53:37.226 --> 01:53:42.244
different ways of participating that are
accepted and valued. Universal supports

01:53:42.245 --> 01:53:47.327
make access more workable for everyone.
Examples include information available in

01:53:47.328 --> 01:53:51.647
more than one form, movement opportunities
and different ways to show

01:53:51.648 --> 01:53:56.730
learning. Targeted supports respond to a
more specific need, such as pre-teaching

01:53:56.731 --> 01:54:01.686
vocabulary for a small group or providing
an organisation checklist. Individual

01:54:01.687 --> 01:54:06.515
adjustments respond to a particular
student's access and participation needs.

01:54:06.516 --> 01:54:11.280
A plan might include a quieter workspace,
additional processing time, a task

01:54:11.281 --> 01:54:16.109
breakdown or another way to demonstrate
learning. The teacher and school work

01:54:16.110 --> 01:54:21.001
through the appropriate planning and
consultation processes. Support staff may

01:54:21.002 --> 01:54:26.021
help deliver support at every layer and
notice how it works across the day. Your

01:54:26.022 --> 01:54:30.913
day map, audit and factual observations
can contribute to review. The decision

01:54:30.914 --> 01:54:35.996
follows the person's access needs and the
evidence; diagnosis alone does not tell

01:54:35.997 --> 01:54:40.952
us which adjustment will help. Think of
the support you give. What is available

01:54:40.953 --> 01:54:45.400
to everyone? What is targeted? What is
individually adjusted? Keep your

01:54:45.401 --> 01:54:48.767
student's experience at the centre of
those questions.

01:54:50.281 --> 01:54:55.393
This slide places reasonable adjustments
within layers of inclusive design. It

01:54:55.394 --> 01:55:00.506
starts with environmental design and
Universal Design for Learning, then names

01:55:00.507 --> 01:55:05.884
quality differentiated teaching practice,
supplementary, substantial and extensive

01:55:05.885 --> 01:55:11.329
adjustments as the four N C C D levels of
adjustment. For this workshop, reasonable

01:55:11.330 --> 01:55:16.575
adjustments are changes or supports that
help students access and participate in

01:55:16.576 --> 01:55:21.821
education in a way that works for them.
They should be individualised, strengths

01:55:21.822 --> 01:55:26.734
based and practical, removing barriers and
enabling participation. Keep the

01:55:26.735 --> 01:55:31.582
distinction clear. Adjustments are the
support actions. The N C C D is the

01:55:31.583 --> 01:55:36.894
process for collecting information about
adjustments. The teacher and school plan

01:55:36.895 --> 01:55:41.874
and review those decisions. You help
deliver many of the supports day to day

01:55:41.875 --> 01:55:46.588
and contribute what you notice. The aim
includes capability, confidence,

01:55:46.589 --> 01:55:51.436
self-advocacy and independence. Follow the
consultation, documentation and

01:55:51.437 --> 01:55:56.416
review processes in your school or system,
and use current official guidance

01:55:56.417 --> 01:55:57.941
for detailed questions.

01:55:59.481 --> 01:56:04.972
Use current official N C C D information
to follow up this part of the workshop.

01:56:04.973 --> 01:56:09.907
The image on this slide is still a
placeholder, and it does not supply a

01:56:09.908 --> 01:56:15.329
finished set of links. Your Participant
Guide points to N C C D dot edu dot au.

01:56:15.330 --> 01:56:19.986
For the support staff role, keep the
contribution clear: you provide

01:56:19.987 --> 01:56:25.478
observations about how adjustments are
working. The teacher and school decide on

01:56:25.479 --> 01:56:30.831
adjustments and N C C D levels. You do not
need access to confidential N C C D

01:56:30.832 --> 01:56:36.253
records to notice and share what helps a
student participate. Know who receives

01:56:36.254 --> 01:56:41.814
your observations and how to pass them on
through your school's agreed process. A

01:56:41.815 --> 01:56:47.166
short note about what happened, what
preceded it and what helped can make that

01:56:47.167 --> 01:56:52.519
conversation specific. We'll develop
communication with the teacher and family

01:56:52.520 --> 01:56:54.186
further in Session Five.

01:56:55.721 --> 01:57:00.887
It's time to see what others have planned.
Keep your private My students

01:57:00.888 --> 01:57:06.055
card with you. Check that your main design
sheet has no student names or

01:57:06.056 --> 01:57:11.513
identifying details before it goes on a
wall. In a group, display the design

01:57:11.514 --> 01:57:17.045
sheets and walk through the gallery. Leave
a note on someone else's plan: one

01:57:17.046 --> 01:57:22.722
idea you would borrow, or one question.
Then return to your own design and read

01:57:22.723 --> 01:57:28.617
what others have left. If you're viewing
alone, you can ask a colleague to look at

01:57:28.618 --> 01:57:33.857
the plan later, or revisit it now with
those same two prompts. What could

01:57:33.858 --> 01:57:39.461
someone borrow? What question still needs
answering? Allow about five minutes.

01:57:39.462 --> 01:57:44.480
Pause the video for the gallery, then
return to refine your next step.

01:57:52.521 --> 01:57:57.071
Take one useful idea or question from that
review. You don't have to use every

01:57:57.072 --> 01:58:01.799
suggestion. Check it against your brief,
your student's experience and the agreed

01:58:01.800 --> 01:58:05.522
supports, then decide what is worth
discussing with the teacher.

01:58:07.041 --> 01:58:12.154
Finish with the Commit box on page
twenty-one and the corresponding lines on

01:58:12.155 --> 01:58:17.133
your private My students card. What
support will you fade first? What will

01:58:17.134 --> 01:58:22.179
you notice? What will you talk to the
teacher about? How will the student's

01:58:22.180 --> 01:58:27.361
view be included, with the teacher? Write
a realistic next step rather than a

01:58:27.362 --> 01:58:32.339
promise to remove help. Keep it within
your role, and take the plan to the

01:58:32.340 --> 01:58:37.522
teacher before changing support. Student
and family knowledge can refine what

01:58:37.523 --> 01:58:42.909
you've noticed and what might be useful to
try. Allow about four minutes to make

01:58:42.910 --> 01:58:47.751
your commitment. You may share a
de-identified version with a partner if

01:58:47.752 --> 01:58:51.297
you wish. Pause here, then resume for the
transition.

01:58:59.321 --> 01:59:04.002
Keep that plan as something to try, review
and refine together. It brings the

01:59:04.003 --> 01:59:08.806
day's understanding into one specific
support moment, while protecting what the

01:59:08.807 --> 01:59:13.426
student still needs. In the next session
we'll work on partnership: a useful

01:59:13.427 --> 01:59:18.231
communication rhythm with the teacher, the
trust families may place in you, and

01:59:18.232 --> 01:59:23.282
how different knowledge can contribute to
decisions. Your teacher conversation from

01:59:23.283 --> 01:59:26.115
this plan gives you a practical place to
begin.

01:59:32.641 --> 01:59:37.546
Welcome to Working in Partnership. In the
last session, you planned a support

01:59:37.547 --> 01:59:42.128
moment and a small step towards
independence. Now we'll consider how the

01:59:42.129 --> 01:59:47.292
people around the student understand and
act together. Have your My students card

01:59:47.293 --> 01:59:52.391
and page twenty-one nearby. Look at what
you will talk to the teacher about, and

01:59:52.392 --> 01:59:57.103
the line, I will talk with. That is our
starting point. You may bring both

01:59:57.104 --> 02:00:02.138
productive and difficult experiences with
families or colleagues. Keep examples

02:00:02.139 --> 02:00:07.173
anonymised and share only what you're
comfortable sharing. The student, family,

02:00:07.174 --> 02:00:11.691
teacher and support staff can each hold
different parts of the picture.

02:00:11.692 --> 02:00:16.338
Partnership belongs in understanding and
planning from the beginning, and

02:00:16.339 --> 02:00:18.660
continues through action and review.

02:00:20.201 --> 02:00:25.443
Look at the overlapping circles. Family,
school staff and support staff each bring

02:00:25.444 --> 02:00:30.557
knowledge and a different relationship
with the student. The student is right at

02:00:30.558 --> 02:00:35.218
the centre. A family may understand
something about recovery after school

02:00:35.219 --> 02:00:40.331
that we don't see in the classroom. An
educator may notice how a particular task

02:00:40.332 --> 02:00:45.445
changes participation. As support staff,
you may bring a close hour-by-hour view

02:00:45.446 --> 02:00:50.623
across different rooms and spaces, along
with your relationships with the student

02:00:50.624 --> 02:00:55.090
and family. Those are possible
contributions, rather than fixed roles.

02:00:55.091 --> 02:01:00.009
No one person has the whole picture,
including us. On page twenty-two, you'll

02:01:00.010 --> 02:01:05.123
find Where partnerships converge. With
someone near you, consider this question:

02:01:05.124 --> 02:01:09.913
what is the goal of these intersecting
partnerships? Then ask, who might be

02:01:09.914 --> 02:01:15.027
missing from this picture? How will the
student communicate their experience and

02:01:15.028 --> 02:01:20.011
preferences? Think back to sensory and
thinking in Session Two, communication,

02:01:20.012 --> 02:01:25.254
connection and identity in Session Three,
and learning design and the continuum of

02:01:25.255 --> 02:01:30.174
support in Session Four. What should these
partnerships make possible for the

02:01:30.175 --> 02:01:35.158
student? Pause the video if you'd like
longer to talk, and then we'll draw the

02:01:35.159 --> 02:01:36.129
ideas together.

02:01:44.160 --> 02:01:49.066
Let's come back to the student at the
centre. A useful shared purpose connects

02:01:49.066 --> 02:01:53.590
our work to their learning, access,
participation and experience. Having

02:01:53.590 --> 02:01:57.604
several people involved doesn't
automatically make a partnership

02:01:57.604 --> 02:02:02.383
effective. Their knowledge needs a way
into the decisions. If you identified

02:02:02.383 --> 02:02:06.971
someone missing, keep that in mind. If the
student's perspective is being

02:02:06.971 --> 02:02:12.068
inferred entirely by adults, consider how
you could check that understanding with

02:02:12.068 --> 02:02:16.719
them. The circles are prompts for thinking
about relationships. They can't

02:02:16.719 --> 02:02:21.498
capture every person or every kind of
knowledge that matters to a particular

02:02:21.498 --> 02:02:22.008
student.

02:02:23.521 --> 02:02:28.212
These six principles come from the
Family-School Partnerships Framework.

02:02:28.213 --> 02:02:32.705
Let's connect each one to what we do.
First, parents and families are

02:02:32.706 --> 02:02:37.000
children's first and continuing educators.
Their knowledge remains

02:02:37.001 --> 02:02:42.022
valuable when a child enters school.
Second, learning is lifelong and happens

02:02:42.023 --> 02:02:47.441
across many settings. The classroom gives
us an important view, but it is one view.

02:02:47.442 --> 02:02:52.727
Third, partnerships are strengthened when
the diversity and strengths of families

02:02:52.728 --> 02:02:58.014
are valued. We need to learn what those
strengths are, rather than assuming every

02:02:58.015 --> 02:03:03.102
family can contribute in the same way.
Fourth, community engagement can expand

02:03:03.103 --> 02:03:08.124
the people, knowledge and resources
involved in learning. Fifth, partnerships

02:03:08.125 --> 02:03:12.815
grow through mutual trust, respect and
responsibility. We can show those

02:03:12.816 --> 02:03:17.375
qualities by listening carefully,
explaining clearly and doing what we

02:03:17.376 --> 02:03:22.265
agree to do. Sixth, partnership needs
committed, collaborative and creative

02:03:22.266 --> 02:03:27.155
leadership. Someone needs to make the time
and processes possible. Everyday

02:03:27.156 --> 02:03:32.044
contact at drop-off, pick-up, the
playground or bus bay can help build that

02:03:32.045 --> 02:03:37.133
trust. In a meeting, these principles
become visible through a shared purpose,

02:03:37.134 --> 02:03:41.824
student communication, accessible
information, and clear next steps. Who

02:03:41.825 --> 02:03:47.111
will do what, and when will we check in
again? Family and school perspectives may

02:03:47.112 --> 02:03:52.265
differ. We can listen and show respect
while we are still working through those

02:03:52.266 --> 02:03:57.419
differences. Bringing those perspectives
together from the beginning gives us a

02:03:57.420 --> 02:03:59.929
better basis for deciding what to try.

02:04:01.440 --> 02:04:06.469
There are many points of collaboration
around an Autistic student: between

02:04:06.470 --> 02:04:11.844
teachers, with a family, with support
staff, with other professionals, and with

02:04:11.845 --> 02:04:17.012
the student themselves. Across those
relationships, the core skills are much

02:04:17.013 --> 02:04:21.766
the same. Before we work through the ideas
on the slide, think about a

02:04:21.767 --> 02:04:27.278
collaboration that helped you understand
something better. What made it possible?

02:04:27.279 --> 02:04:32.515
Perhaps there was a clear purpose, time to
ask questions, or a willingness to

02:04:32.516 --> 02:04:37.683
change an initial view. At your table,
name two or three principles that you

02:04:37.684 --> 02:04:43.127
think matter most. You can also consider
the question on the slide: how could we

02:04:43.128 --> 02:04:47.743
work together for better learning
outcomes? Keep the student in that

02:04:47.744 --> 02:04:52.290
conversation. Pause the video for a longer
discussion, then resume.

02:05:00.321 --> 02:05:05.248
Let's bring those principles together.
Shared goals help us understand what

02:05:05.249 --> 02:05:10.642
we're working towards, and shared values
help us understand why it matters. Mutual

02:05:10.643 --> 02:05:15.836
trust and respect need to be present in
the way we speak and listen. Consulting

02:05:15.837 --> 02:05:21.230
on decisions means leaving room for what
we hear to influence the decision. Seeing

02:05:21.231 --> 02:05:25.825
another perspective and inviting curiosity
help us notice what our own

02:05:25.826 --> 02:05:30.819
position might hide. Centring student
voice reminds us whose experience this

02:05:30.820 --> 02:05:35.947
work concerns. Assuming good intent gives
us a constructive starting point. We

02:05:35.948 --> 02:05:40.875
still need to attend to the effects of our
actions, and to concerns someone

02:05:40.876 --> 02:05:46.335
raises. A useful question is, what can we
learn from each other that will help this

02:05:46.336 --> 02:05:51.196
student? These principles apply whoever is
at the table, including in your

02:05:51.197 --> 02:05:53.725
working relationship with the teacher.

02:05:55.240 --> 02:06:00.393
Short, regular and planned communication
works better than waiting until something

02:06:00.394 --> 02:06:05.483
goes wrong. A few minutes at a set time
can help you and the teacher use what you

02:06:05.484 --> 02:06:10.255
both know. In a primary setting, that
might be a brief check at the end of a

02:06:10.256 --> 02:06:15.409
particular lesson each week. In secondary
school, you might begin with one subject

02:06:15.410 --> 02:06:20.372
teacher and one regular opportunity. Time
isn't always built into support staff

02:06:20.373 --> 02:06:25.335
hours, so choose something realistic and
discuss how to make it possible. Bring

02:06:25.336 --> 02:06:30.298
three things: what you saw, what helped,
and one question. Keep the observation

02:06:30.299 --> 02:06:35.197
separate from your interpretation. Work
from the teacher's plan for the lesson

02:06:35.198 --> 02:06:40.288
and student. If there isn't a clear plan
yet, ask, 'What should I know to support

02:06:40.289 --> 02:06:45.442
them well?' Agree a first step instead of
making up a new approach alone. You help

02:06:45.443 --> 02:06:50.277
deliver adjustments. The teacher plans
them with you. Your close observations

02:06:50.278 --> 02:06:54.858
can make that planning more informed. Take
a minute to note one realistic

02:06:54.859 --> 02:06:59.758
check-in time and one question you would
bring. You can start with the teacher

02:06:59.759 --> 02:07:03.830
conversation on your Session Four plan.
Pause the video to write.

02:07:11.840 --> 02:07:16.791
Look at the time and question you've
chosen. Is it a manageable place to

02:07:16.792 --> 02:07:22.302
begin? A missing plan is a reason to ask,
not to criticise the teacher. Keep the

02:07:22.303 --> 02:07:27.812
conversation collegial and specific: what
you noticed, what helped, and what you

02:07:27.813 --> 02:07:29.486
need to understand next.

02:07:31.000 --> 02:07:35.832
Families may tell you something first
because of the trust built in everyday

02:07:35.833 --> 02:07:40.924
contact. You might see them at the gate,
pick-up, the playground or bus bay, and

02:07:40.925 --> 02:07:45.821
you know their child. That trust is a
strength. The guide also recognises the

02:07:45.822 --> 02:07:50.655
important links that support staff can
hold between families and teachers in

02:07:50.656 --> 02:07:55.166
First Nations communities. Local roles and
relationships differ. Follow

02:07:55.167 --> 02:07:59.935
culturally informed guidance in your
setting, and make space for colleagues

02:07:59.936 --> 02:08:04.639
who wish to contribute without asking
anyone to speak for their community.

02:08:04.640 --> 02:08:09.537
Knowing your school's pathway helps you
use that trust well. Receive what the

02:08:09.538 --> 02:08:14.048
family shares, acknowledge it and bring
the appropriate person into the

02:08:14.049 --> 02:08:19.268
conversation. You don't have to carry the
concern alone. Anything raising a safety

02:08:19.269 --> 02:08:24.037
concern follows the school's child
protection procedures straight away. The

02:08:24.038 --> 02:08:28.806
warm handover on the slide is, 'That
matters. Can we bring the teacher into

02:08:28.807 --> 02:08:33.768
this together?' Try saying that quietly or
to a colleague. It acknowledges the

02:08:33.769 --> 02:08:38.731
concern and invites a shared next step.
Tell the family what will happen next,

02:08:38.732 --> 02:08:43.886
and follow up. Don't promise an outcome or
confidentiality you can't provide. You

02:08:43.887 --> 02:08:48.784
only need the information relevant to your
role; this is not an invitation to

02:08:48.785 --> 02:08:53.746
seek more private detail. Now note who you
would approach first in your school

02:08:53.747 --> 02:08:58.773
and one part of the pathway you need to
check. Use a general or made-up example

02:08:58.774 --> 02:09:03.607
rather than a real family story. Pause
here to practise the wording and make

02:09:03.608 --> 02:09:04.250
your note.

02:09:12.280 --> 02:09:16.905
Let's return. The school's pathway should
support the relationship the family has

02:09:16.906 --> 02:09:21.184
with you. Keep the next step clear,
including who will be involved and what

02:09:21.185 --> 02:09:25.521
the family can expect to hear back.
Bringing the teacher in thoughtfully can

02:09:25.522 --> 02:09:29.800
preserve trust while making sure the
concern reaches someone who can act on

02:09:29.801 --> 02:09:29.972
it.

02:09:31.480 --> 02:09:36.892
The seesaw invites us to notice power
between school, home and the student.

02:09:36.893 --> 02:09:42.450
Power can sit in income and resources,
past experiences of education, culture

02:09:42.451 --> 02:09:47.936
and language, or knowledge of school
systems and curriculum. It can also sit

02:09:47.937 --> 02:09:53.934
in what each person believes about school
and professional authority. Support staff

02:09:53.935 --> 02:09:59.273
can see power from more than one position.
A family may see you as part of

02:09:59.274 --> 02:10:05.125
the school, while inside the school you
may have less say in a decision. That can

02:10:05.126 --> 02:10:10.976
help you notice an imbalance others miss.
We work within education every day. Its

02:10:10.977 --> 02:10:16.169
language and routines can feel ordinary to
us, while being unfamiliar or

02:10:16.170 --> 02:10:21.875
intimidating to someone else. Think about
an agenda full of acronyms, a meeting

02:10:21.876 --> 02:10:27.068
time that is hard to attend, or a decision
presented as already settled.

02:10:27.069 --> 02:10:32.334
Ask yourself: who sets the agenda? Whose
knowledge is heard? Who can make

02:10:32.335 --> 02:10:38.039
decisions? Is the student included in a
way that works for them? And what could

02:10:38.040 --> 02:10:43.671
the school change about the process? These
questions help us examine practice.

02:10:43.672 --> 02:10:49.376
They aren't an invitation to assign blame
to an individual. Before describing a

02:10:49.377 --> 02:10:54.862
family as difficult or disengaged, we need
to look carefully at the context,

02:10:54.863 --> 02:11:00.640
the relationship and the opportunities
they have been offered to contribute. Our

02:11:00.641 --> 02:11:05.101
intention matters, and so does how the
process is experienced.

02:11:06.640 --> 02:11:11.561
Let's explore that on page twenty-three,
under Power dynamics. You'll need three

02:11:11.562 --> 02:11:16.607
sticky notes, or three spaces on a page.
On the first, write an example of a power

02:11:16.608 --> 02:11:21.528
imbalance from a home perspective. On the
second, write an example from a school

02:11:21.529 --> 02:11:26.325
perspective. On the third, write how those
imbalances could be addressed. Keep

02:11:26.326 --> 02:11:30.748
the people involved anonymous. You might
think about language, access to

02:11:30.749 --> 02:11:35.483
information, who attends a meeting, or who
gets to define the problem. School

02:11:35.484 --> 02:11:40.529
acronyms such as NAPLAN, NCCD and IEP are
familiar to many educators, but we can't

02:11:40.530 --> 02:11:45.263
assume they are equally accessible to
families. Professional status, cultural

02:11:45.264 --> 02:11:49.624
expectations and the involvement of
external providers can also shape a

02:11:49.625 --> 02:11:54.172
meeting. Share your notes with your table.
If you would welcome a movement

02:11:54.173 --> 02:11:58.657
break, you could share with someone
elsewhere in the room, or place notes

02:11:58.658 --> 02:12:03.081
under Home, School and Solutions on a
wall. The live activity allows six

02:12:03.082 --> 02:12:07.753
minutes. Pause here to use that time. Look
for something that could make the

02:12:07.754 --> 02:12:10.493
process fairer, and resume when you're
ready.

02:12:18.520 --> 02:12:23.055
Let's come back together. Look at the
changes you've proposed through four

02:12:23.056 --> 02:12:27.343
questions. Who sets the agenda? Whose
knowledge is heard? Who can make

02:12:27.344 --> 02:12:31.569
decisions? What can the school change
about the process? Power can be

02:12:31.570 --> 02:12:35.919
structural, historical and cultural. It
can also operate without anyone

02:12:35.920 --> 02:12:40.891
consciously intending it. A difference in
status may feel significant even when a

02:12:40.892 --> 02:12:45.427
meeting has been organised with good
intentions. Choose one process change

02:12:45.428 --> 02:12:50.337
from your notes. It could be a change to
preparation, communication, the meeting

02:12:50.338 --> 02:12:55.370
itself or follow-up. Keep it beside your
student planning. We'll carry it into the

02:12:55.371 --> 02:13:00.093
bridge reflection at the end of this
session, and then into your conversation

02:13:00.094 --> 02:13:01.336
plan in Session Six.

02:13:02.840 --> 02:13:08.207
We're going to hear an example involving
Adon, where decisions were made without

02:13:08.208 --> 02:13:13.168
the student being listened to or
consulted. As you watch, pay attention to

02:13:13.169 --> 02:13:18.196
what decisions were made, and who made
them. Then consider the impact those

02:13:18.197 --> 02:13:23.224
decisions had on Adon. There's space to
reflect under Student voice on page

02:13:23.225 --> 02:13:24.990
twenty-three. Let's watch.

02:15:17.440 --> 02:15:22.260
What impact did those decisions have on
Adon? Take a moment to consider what you

02:15:22.261 --> 02:15:27.142
heard, and what might have been different
if Adon's perspective had been included

02:15:27.143 --> 02:15:31.903
in the decisions. Now bring the question
back to the students you have in mind.

02:15:31.904 --> 02:15:36.602
How are their preferences asked for and
heard? Is there a way for a student to

02:15:36.603 --> 02:15:41.545
tell you that something isn't working, as
well as to choose between options adults

02:15:41.546 --> 02:15:46.000
have already offered? Pause here if you'd
like time to reflect or discuss.

02:15:54.001 --> 02:15:58.883
Students need a say in decisions about
them, in ways that work with their

02:15:58.884 --> 02:16:04.173
communication. Even a young child can
express a preference. We can offer visual

02:16:04.174 --> 02:16:09.734
choices, attend to a response, and honour
what it tells us. Questions such as, what

02:16:09.735 --> 02:16:14.956
helps, what's hard, and what would you
like teachers to know, can open up that

02:16:14.957 --> 02:16:20.382
understanding. We may need to change the
format, the setting or the time we allow

02:16:20.383 --> 02:16:25.265
for an answer. Where a student uses words,
record their words accurately.

02:16:25.266 --> 02:16:30.283
Where they communicate in other ways, make
clear what they communicated and

02:16:30.284 --> 02:16:35.573
how. Keep adult interpretation visible as
interpretation. Pass what the student

02:16:35.574 --> 02:16:40.592
tells you to the teacher through the
agreed pathway. Support staff may hear

02:16:40.593 --> 02:16:45.475
preferences during the day that would
otherwise be missed. Including that

02:16:45.476 --> 02:16:50.358
perspective in the record, and acting on
it, helps build a foundation for

02:16:50.359 --> 02:16:51.306
self-advocacy.

02:16:52.840 --> 02:16:57.730
Turn to the Partnership activity grid on
page twenty-four. This gives us a way to

02:16:57.731 --> 02:17:02.682
look at the people in a partnership with a
little more care. Work through the four

02:17:02.683 --> 02:17:07.206
partners: the student, parents and carers,
teachers, and support staff. For

02:17:07.207 --> 02:17:11.670
each, note what they bring and the
challenges they might face. Think about

02:17:11.671 --> 02:17:16.072
knowledge, relationships, time,
communication and the demands each person

02:17:16.073 --> 02:17:20.412
may be managing. Avoid assuming that every
person in a role has the same

02:17:20.413 --> 02:17:25.059
experience. Give the Support staff row the
same care as the others. You might

02:17:25.060 --> 02:17:29.033
include your hour-by-hour view,
relationships with the student and

02:17:29.034 --> 02:17:33.924
family, or knowledge of what works across
settings. Keep the Teachers row focused

02:17:33.925 --> 02:17:38.448
on workload and systems, rather than
criticism of individuals. Then compare

02:17:38.449 --> 02:17:42.972
your four sections. What do they have in
common? Where do you see different

02:17:42.973 --> 02:17:47.863
knowledge or different needs? What could
help them work together? Give yourselves

02:17:47.864 --> 02:17:52.327
eight minutes for the full activity.
Everyone can contribute in a way that

02:17:52.328 --> 02:17:57.279
suits them, and you can write or reflect
quietly as well as talk. Keep the student

02:17:57.280 --> 02:18:01.802
at the centre of the grid. Pause the video
now, and return for the debrief.

02:18:09.840 --> 02:18:14.984
Let's draw that together. Begin with what
you wrote in the Support staff row. What

02:18:14.984 --> 02:18:19.811
strengths and knowledge did you identify,
alongside any challenges? Then look

02:18:19.811 --> 02:18:24.320
across your grid for similarities and
differences. Which strengths might

02:18:24.320 --> 02:18:28.956
complement one another? Which challenges
could the partnership itself help

02:18:28.956 --> 02:18:33.529
address? A useful next question is, what
would make this partnership more

02:18:33.529 --> 02:18:38.546
successful? Perhaps you need a clearer
shared purpose, a different communication

02:18:38.546 --> 02:18:43.373
arrangement, or a way for one person's
knowledge to be heard earlier. Use the

02:18:43.373 --> 02:18:48.454
circumstances you've identified to guide
that choice. Return to our collaboration

02:18:48.454 --> 02:18:52.773
principles: shared goals and values, trust
and respect, consultation,

02:18:52.773 --> 02:18:57.282
curiosity and student voice. Assuming good
intent about outcomes for the

02:18:57.282 --> 02:19:01.537
student can help us begin, while listening
tells us what each person

02:19:01.537 --> 02:19:05.792
actually needs and hopes for.
Understanding each partner's strengths

02:19:05.792 --> 02:19:10.490
and challenges gives trust and confidence
something practical to grow from.

02:19:12.000 --> 02:19:17.052
Let's draw on relationships that already
work. Still on page twenty-four, find

02:19:17.053 --> 02:19:22.171
What works? In your school group, think of
a family with whom you have, or have

02:19:22.172 --> 02:19:27.159
had, a good relationship. How did you
establish it? What does it look like in

02:19:27.160 --> 02:19:31.884
practice? Why does it work? Try to
identify the actual moves that helped:

02:19:31.885 --> 02:19:37.003
what someone did before a meeting, how
information was shared, or how a concern

02:19:37.004 --> 02:19:42.318
was handled. Keep the example anonymous,
and stay with what you can learn from the

02:19:42.319 --> 02:19:46.978
relationship. If you're working alone,
make a few notes against the same

02:19:46.979 --> 02:19:51.375
questions. The activity allows six
minutes. Pause the video for that

02:19:51.376 --> 02:19:55.903
reflection and conversation. When you
return, we'll connect it to some

02:19:55.904 --> 02:19:58.856
practices that can help everyone at the
table.

02:20:06.880 --> 02:20:11.982
Let's come back. Choose one idea from your
reflection to carry into another

02:20:11.982 --> 02:20:16.947
relationship. The list gives us practical
starting points: prepare, share

02:20:16.947 --> 02:20:21.773
information, ask questions, seek
clarification and assume the best. For

02:20:21.773 --> 02:20:26.255
your role, preparation can include
agreeing with the teacher which

02:20:26.255 --> 02:20:31.150
observations you will share. Use the
agreed communication pathway so the

02:20:31.150 --> 02:20:36.184
teacher and family hear the same
information. Ask for clarification when a

02:20:36.184 --> 02:20:41.700
role or plan is unclear, and begin with a
strength or something that went well in

02:20:41.700 --> 02:20:47.009
the day. A family may bring a support
person. Make room for that participation

02:20:47.009 --> 02:20:52.318
within the meeting arrangements. If a
conversation has gone badly, acknowledge

02:20:52.318 --> 02:20:57.765
it, offer a reset and bring the teacher
into the next step. Families continue to

02:20:57.765 --> 02:21:03.074
support learning across settings. Their
knowledge belongs in the work, and the

02:21:03.074 --> 02:21:07.761
trust you build in everyday moments can
help that knowledge be heard.

02:21:09.280 --> 02:21:14.561
Meeting families where they are begins
with listening before solving. Ask about

02:21:14.562 --> 02:21:20.046
communication preferences and priorities.
Make room for questions and uncertainty,

02:21:20.047 --> 02:21:24.718
including questions we haven't
anticipated. Explain roles, options and

02:21:24.719 --> 02:21:29.999
next steps in plain language. Check what
is workable for the family now. A plan

02:21:30.000 --> 02:21:34.807
that looks simple within a school
timetable may interact with demands we

02:21:34.808 --> 02:21:40.292
don't know about. Avoid judging the pace
of a response, the choices someone makes,

02:21:40.293 --> 02:21:45.506
or the time they need to consider an
option. This brings our environment-first

02:21:45.507 --> 02:21:50.720
thinking into the meeting itself. Where
might the school's language, timing or

02:21:50.721 --> 02:21:56.069
process create a barrier? What could we
change? A different format, questions in

02:21:56.070 --> 02:22:01.080
advance, a quieter place, or clearer
follow-up may change the experience of

02:22:01.081 --> 02:22:06.497
consultation. Keep asking what you need to
understand. Listening and clarity give

02:22:06.498 --> 02:22:09.883
us a stronger basis for solving something
together.

02:22:11.401 --> 02:22:16.304
On page twenty-five you'll find the
Consultation checklist. You can use it

02:22:16.305 --> 02:22:21.141
before a meeting, and return to it
afterwards. Start with a clear, shared

02:22:21.142 --> 02:22:26.448
purpose. What are we trying to understand
or decide together? Use open questions

02:22:26.449 --> 02:22:31.553
that allow an answer you haven't already
chosen. Provide more than one way to

02:22:31.554 --> 02:22:37.062
contribute, and respect the knowledge the
student and family bring. Record what you

02:22:37.063 --> 02:22:42.503
hear accurately. For support staff, this
includes what a student or family said in

02:22:42.504 --> 02:22:47.810
their own words, passed on through the
agreed pathway. It can help to check your

02:22:47.811 --> 02:22:53.117
understanding aloud: have I understood
that correctly? Then agree an action, who

02:22:53.118 --> 02:22:58.357
is responsible and how you will follow up.
If something remains undecided, make

02:22:58.358 --> 02:23:03.395
that clear too. Afterwards, ask yourself
whether the consultation changed or

02:23:03.396 --> 02:23:08.433
extended your understanding. Could
everyone contribute in a workable way? Do

02:23:08.434 --> 02:23:13.203
people know what happens next? Ask, listen
and follow up. A checklist or

02:23:13.204 --> 02:23:17.972
planning tool can help us remember those
steps, while the quality of the

02:23:17.973 --> 02:23:22.406
consultation still depends on our
attention to the people involved.

02:23:23.920 --> 02:23:29.050
Let's look first at consulting students.
Begin by presuming competence, and make

02:23:29.051 --> 02:23:33.727
communication accessible. That might
involve augmentative and alternative

02:23:33.728 --> 02:23:38.793
communication, visuals, writing, drawing,
video or symbols. Offer questions and

02:23:38.794 --> 02:23:43.340
meeting information beforehand, simple
choices where useful, and enough

02:23:43.341 --> 02:23:47.887
processing time. Notice the sensory
environment. Build on the student's

02:23:47.888 --> 02:23:53.018
strengths and interests. Behaviour may
communicate preferences or difficulty, so

02:23:53.019 --> 02:23:57.760
observe carefully and check your
interpretation wherever possible. Trusted

02:23:57.761 --> 02:24:02.826
supporters can assist communication while
keeping the student's own perspective

02:24:02.827 --> 02:24:07.892
central. If you sit beside a student in a
meeting, help them communicate rather

02:24:07.893 --> 02:24:12.959
than answering for them. What a student
tells us needs to influence what we do.

02:24:12.960 --> 02:24:17.830
With families, begin with the student's
strengths, interests and what works.

02:24:17.831 --> 02:24:21.987
Listen to their knowledge without assuming
you already know their

02:24:21.988 --> 02:24:27.053
experience. Use plain language, explain
school terms and respect differences in

02:24:27.054 --> 02:24:32.185
culture and perspective. Some parents may
be neurodivergent themselves, so check

02:24:32.186 --> 02:24:36.991
communication preferences rather than
making assumptions. Use real examples

02:24:36.992 --> 02:24:41.863
from school to discuss needs, goals,
participation and possible adjustments.

02:24:41.864 --> 02:24:46.864
Check shared understanding, agree next
steps and keep the conversation open as

02:24:46.865 --> 02:24:51.541
circumstances change. This extends the
consultation work we introduced in

02:24:51.542 --> 02:24:56.542
Session Four. Choose one practice from
each column that you could try with the

02:24:56.543 --> 02:25:00.699
student and family you have in mind. Pause
to note those choices.

02:25:08.720 --> 02:25:13.181
As you look at your two choices, check
what they would make easier for the

02:25:13.182 --> 02:25:17.399
person involved. Can the student
communicate more comfortably? Can the

02:25:17.400 --> 02:25:22.166
family prepare, ask a question or add
knowledge that might otherwise be missed?

02:25:22.167 --> 02:25:27.056
Keep those choices with your conversation
notes. Consultation becomes more useful

02:25:27.057 --> 02:25:31.335
when we adapt it to the people involved,
and then act on what we learn.

02:25:32.840 --> 02:25:37.497
Partnership becomes particularly important
when a student's capacity runs

02:25:37.498 --> 02:25:42.026
out. The terms on this slide are meltdown,
shutdown, and what the slide

02:25:42.027 --> 02:25:47.202
calls School Can't. That is, the student
cannot attend school. We are considering

02:25:47.203 --> 02:25:52.054
these through capacity, accumulated
demands and the parts of experience that

02:25:52.055 --> 02:25:56.971
may be difficult for others to see. These
experiences need a response without

02:25:56.972 --> 02:26:01.112
blame. Describing them as wilful
misbehaviour, or as a failure of

02:26:01.113 --> 02:26:05.447
parenting, can close down the
understanding we need. Home and school

02:26:05.448 --> 02:26:10.040
may see different parts of the student's
day. A difference between those

02:26:10.041 --> 02:26:15.151
observations is a reason to listen and
learn more. Bring back the questions from

02:26:15.152 --> 02:26:20.133
Sessions Two and Three. What has the day
been asking of this student? What may

02:26:20.134 --> 02:26:25.115
have accumulated? What information does
the student have, and what might their

02:26:25.116 --> 02:26:29.644
family know about recovery? Keep the focus
on safety, what preceded the

02:26:29.645 --> 02:26:34.690
situation, what supports recovery, and
what can change in the environment, task

02:26:34.691 --> 02:26:39.607
or response. Support staff may notice
early signs and be nearby when capacity

02:26:39.608 --> 02:26:44.459
runs out. Your observations of what came
before and what helped recovery are

02:26:44.460 --> 02:26:49.441
useful to the teacher and family. Follow
the student's plan and school policy;

02:26:49.442 --> 02:26:54.035
this is support and recovery, not physical
intervention. The partnership

02:26:54.036 --> 02:26:58.369
task here is to share information
respectfully, listen carefully and

02:26:58.370 --> 02:27:03.350
identify changes that may reduce demands
and improve the student's experience.

02:27:04.880 --> 02:27:10.109
Here are two Positive Partnerships tools
that can help make those conversations

02:27:10.110 --> 02:27:15.137
concrete. On the left, the Working
Together Communication Tool asks how home

02:27:15.138 --> 02:27:20.367
and school will communicate, how often,
about what, and what the next steps and

02:27:20.368 --> 02:27:25.395
dates will be. You can discuss options
such as text, email, an app, phone, a

02:27:25.396 --> 02:27:30.290
communication book or face-to-face
conversation. Choose what is useful and

02:27:30.291 --> 02:27:35.318
workable together. On the right, the
Partnership Planning Template asks what

02:27:35.319 --> 02:27:40.615
has worked, what has been challenging and
what could help. It gives examples and

02:27:40.616 --> 02:27:45.577
space for your own ideas. You might use it
to explore meeting arrangements,

02:27:45.578 --> 02:27:51.075
feeling heard, communication or including
the young person's voice. For example, if

02:27:51.076 --> 02:27:56.036
a family says phone calls are hard to
manage during work, you could use the

02:27:56.037 --> 02:28:01.199
tools to discuss another option and agree
when it will be reviewed. That is an

02:28:01.200 --> 02:28:06.227
illustrative use of the prompts; the
family may suggest something different.

02:28:06.228 --> 02:28:11.390
Use the tools with people. Leave room for
their priorities to change what gets

02:28:11.391 --> 02:28:16.619
written. A completed page is useful when
it records a shared understanding that

02:28:16.620 --> 02:28:18.765
can guide the next conversation.

02:28:20.280 --> 02:28:25.517
The NCCD Meetings Toolkit is another
practical resource. NCCD stands for the

02:28:25.518 --> 02:28:31.034
Nationally Consistent Collection of Data
on School Students with Disability. You

02:28:31.035 --> 02:28:36.691
can find the toolkit on the official NCCD
website. Its sections support requesting

02:28:36.692 --> 02:28:41.999
and managing meetings, being prepared,
managing emotions, keeping records and

02:28:42.000 --> 02:28:47.027
considering whether a meeting was
successful. The teacher and school lead

02:28:47.028 --> 02:28:51.916
this process. Your observations and
records of the support you give can

02:28:51.917 --> 02:28:57.503
contribute to the evidence they keep. The
toolkit helps make the meeting purpose,

02:28:57.504 --> 02:29:02.112
consultation process, possible
adjustments, evidence and next steps

02:29:02.113 --> 02:29:07.281
clear. Explain responsibilities and
follow-up in a way that the student and

02:29:07.282 --> 02:29:12.588
family can understand. People need to know
why a meeting is happening, who is

02:29:12.589 --> 02:29:17.966
involved and how they can contribute.
Follow the processes used in your school

02:29:17.967 --> 02:29:23.134
or system, and use current official NCCD
information when you need specific

02:29:23.135 --> 02:29:28.442
guidance. The toolkit gives you a way to
prepare and review the process while

02:29:28.443 --> 02:29:32.632
keeping the discussion connected to the
student's experience.

02:29:34.160 --> 02:29:39.624
There are also practical support pathways
beyond today's workshop. Page thirty of

02:29:39.625 --> 02:29:44.679
your Participant Guide brings the
resources together. Positive Partnerships

02:29:44.680 --> 02:29:49.938
provides resources and learning options
for educators and families. This slide

02:29:49.939 --> 02:29:55.335
also shows Autism Connect, the national
autism information helpline, with phone,

02:29:55.336 --> 02:30:00.594
email and webchat options. Disability
Gateway can help people with disability,

02:30:00.595 --> 02:30:06.059
families and carers find information and
services. Carer Gateway provides support

02:30:06.060 --> 02:30:11.387
for carers. The contact details are on the
slide, so pause if you would like to

02:30:11.388 --> 02:30:16.510
record them. Choose a pathway that fits
the person's question, and check its

02:30:16.511 --> 02:30:21.770
website for the available support. We can
make families aware of these options

02:30:21.771 --> 02:30:24.639
while listening to what they want and
need.

02:30:27.641 --> 02:30:32.289
Before we finish this session, let's
prepare the bridge to one purposeful

02:30:32.290 --> 02:30:37.390
conversation. Return to the student you
have in mind and the support you plan to

02:30:37.391 --> 02:30:42.427
fade on your My students card. Open page
twenty-five, Bridge to the next family

02:30:42.428 --> 02:30:46.882
conversation. Work through these questions
privately. What needs to be

02:30:46.883 --> 02:30:51.725
understood? What question will open the
conversation? How will the student's

02:30:51.726 --> 02:30:56.633
voice be included? What can be decided
together? How and when will the school

02:30:56.634 --> 02:31:01.928
follow up? The conversation can be with a
family or with the student. If it is with

02:31:01.929 --> 02:31:06.835
a family, plan how the teacher will be
part of it. The warm handover may give

02:31:06.836 --> 02:31:11.291
you a useful opening. Bring in the process
change you chose during the

02:31:11.292 --> 02:31:16.521
power-dynamics activity. Make it part of
how the conversation will happen, as well

02:31:16.522 --> 02:31:21.364
as what you will ask. You are preparing
your own purpose and approach. Leave

02:31:21.365 --> 02:31:26.142
space for the student's and family's
knowledge to change your understanding

02:31:26.143 --> 02:31:31.373
and the next step. Keep names out of your
workshop notes. Allow about four minutes

02:31:31.374 --> 02:31:36.602
to write. Pause the video now, and resume
when you're ready to close this session.

02:31:44.641 --> 02:31:49.504
Take a final look at the question you plan
to open with. Does it invite the

02:31:49.505 --> 02:31:54.565
other person's knowledge? Have you left
something meaningful to understand and

02:31:54.566 --> 02:31:59.494
decide together? Keep these notes. In
Session Six, you'll use them to plan a

02:31:59.495 --> 02:32:04.423
real conversation on page twenty-eight,
alongside one action in your support

02:32:04.424 --> 02:32:09.682
role. The person you named under I will
talk with in Session Four now has a place

02:32:09.683 --> 02:32:14.480
in that plan. We'll bring the day together
through belonging, capacity and

02:32:14.481 --> 02:32:19.672
joy, and choose the practical next steps
you want to carry back to school. Thank

02:32:19.673 --> 02:32:24.206
you for the care you've brought to
thinking about these relationships.

02:32:30.721 --> 02:32:35.920
Welcome to our final session, Flourishing
and Full Circle. We're going to return to

02:32:35.921 --> 02:32:40.740
the students and questions you began with,
and turn your thinking into a next

02:32:40.741 --> 02:32:45.686
step you can use. Have your My students
card and your partnership notes nearby.

02:32:45.687 --> 02:32:50.569
By the end of this session, you'll have
one action for your next day in school

02:32:50.570 --> 02:32:55.198
and one purposeful conversation with a
family or student, planned with the

02:32:55.199 --> 02:32:59.827
teacher where needed to take forward.
We'll also make time for reflection,

02:32:59.828 --> 02:33:04.710
resources and feedback. You don't need to
carry away an unmanageable list. One

02:33:04.711 --> 02:33:09.847
thoughtful action, informed by the people
involved and open to review, is a useful

02:33:09.848 --> 02:33:10.797
place to begin.

02:33:12.320 --> 02:33:17.727
Belonging, capacity and joy bring the
threads of today together. Keep all three

02:33:17.728 --> 02:33:23.204
in the picture when you consider whether a
change is helping a student flourish.

02:33:23.205 --> 02:33:27.919
Belonging means being recognised, included
and able to participate as

02:33:27.920 --> 02:33:33.257
oneself. Capacity asks us to consider what
the day costs, what it protects and

02:33:33.258 --> 02:33:38.388
what helps restore energy. Joy includes
interests, connection and authentic

02:33:38.389 --> 02:33:43.172
expression. These ideas connect our
neurodiversity-affirming lens, our

02:33:43.173 --> 02:33:48.649
support design and our partnerships. We
begin with the person, learn about their

02:33:48.650 --> 02:33:54.057
experience, and bring student, family,
teacher and support staff knowledge into

02:33:54.058 --> 02:33:59.534
the decisions. Look again at the support
you plan to fade or change from Session

02:33:59.535 --> 02:34:04.248
Four. It may reduce a barrier to
participation. Could it also support

02:34:04.249 --> 02:34:09.102
belonging or joy? How might it affect what
the day asks of the student?

02:34:09.103 --> 02:34:14.094
Remember the questions from Session Three:
what costs, and what lightens?

02:34:14.095 --> 02:34:19.363
Academic progress can be part of the
picture, but it won't tell us everything

02:34:19.364 --> 02:34:24.355
about a student's experience. Quiet
compliance or doing something without

02:34:24.356 --> 02:34:29.346
support is not, by itself, evidence of
flourishing. Keep returning to the

02:34:29.347 --> 02:34:34.616
student, and to belonging, capacity and
joy, as you judge what your change is

02:34:34.617 --> 02:34:35.725
making possible.

02:34:37.240 --> 02:34:42.209
Let's bring the strongest ideas together
at your table. Open page twenty-six of

02:34:42.210 --> 02:34:47.051
your Participant Guide. Each person has
about a minute to share the strongest

02:34:47.052 --> 02:34:51.702
thing they're taking back to school.
Choose something that has stayed with

02:34:51.703 --> 02:34:56.099
you: an insight, a question or a practical
change. You can draw on the

02:34:56.100 --> 02:35:01.069
gallery walk from Session Four as well as
your own planning. Then make a shared

02:35:01.070 --> 02:35:06.039
shortlist of three ideas from the table
that are most worth spreading. For each

02:35:06.040 --> 02:35:11.072
idea, name who needs to hear it, and the
meeting or moment where you could share

02:35:11.073 --> 02:35:15.468
it. Be as concrete as you can. A
particular colleague and a particular

02:35:15.469 --> 02:35:20.566
opportunity will help you see what to do
next. That might be the teacher you work

02:35:20.567 --> 02:35:25.472
with, a learning support team meeting or a
support staff meeting. The activity

02:35:25.473 --> 02:35:30.187
allows eight minutes altogether. If you're
working independently, make your

02:35:30.188 --> 02:35:34.965
own shortlist and identify who you could
discuss it with. Pause here to work

02:35:34.966 --> 02:35:38.215
through the activity, then resume for the
next step.

02:35:46.240 --> 02:35:50.857
Let's return to your shortlist. Check that
each idea has somewhere to go: a

02:35:50.858 --> 02:35:55.600
person who needs to hear it, and a real
opportunity to talk. If an idea still

02:35:55.601 --> 02:36:00.529
feels broad, add one example of what it
could mean in practice. You can keep the

02:36:00.530 --> 02:36:04.960
other ideas for later. For now, we're
going to make your own next action

02:36:04.961 --> 02:36:08.205
specific enough to begin, and clear enough
to review.

02:36:09.720 --> 02:36:15.345
Turn to Start, Continue, Reconsider on
page twenty-seven. Plan your next day in

02:36:15.346 --> 02:36:20.322
school. Have your Session Four My students
card nearby, especially the

02:36:20.323 --> 02:36:25.948
line, one way I will fade support. Under
Start, choose one small step you could

02:36:25.949 --> 02:36:31.286
begin next week. Your agreed fading step
may fit here. Under Continue, name

02:36:31.287 --> 02:36:36.696
something today confirmed you already do
well. Give that real attention: the

02:36:36.697 --> 02:36:41.889
workshop may have put words around a
thoughtful practice you already use.

02:36:41.890 --> 02:36:47.371
Under Reconsider, name an approach you
would now look at differently, without

02:36:47.372 --> 02:36:53.070
turning reflection into criticism of a
colleague, family or school. How will you

02:36:53.071 --> 02:36:58.696
know it worked? What evidence will show
that? These stems may help: I will. The

02:36:58.697 --> 02:37:04.466
support or demand I am addressing is. I
will notice. I will talk with the teacher

02:37:04.467 --> 02:37:10.237
about. We will review this on. Keep the
action within your role and the teacher's

02:37:10.238 --> 02:37:15.286
plan. If it changes that plan, the teacher
conversation comes first. An

02:37:15.287 --> 02:37:20.984
action requiring a student to appear less
Autistic needs rethinking. Allow eight

02:37:20.985 --> 02:37:26.683
minutes. This is your card. The suggested
next step is sharing one thing from it

02:37:26.684 --> 02:37:32.309
with the teacher you work with; anything
beyond that is optional. Pause here to

02:37:32.310 --> 02:37:35.121
write, then return to check the action.

02:37:43.160 --> 02:37:48.340
Let's look once more at your Start. Is it
small enough to begin? Does it name a

02:37:48.341 --> 02:37:53.521
support or demand you can act on within
your role? Have you identified what you

02:37:53.522 --> 02:37:58.769
will discuss with the teacher, how the
student’s view will be included, and when

02:37:58.770 --> 02:38:04.017
you will review what happens together?
Check the evidence you plan to notice. If

02:38:04.018 --> 02:38:09.264
the measure is simply that the student
becomes quieter or complies more readily,

02:38:09.265 --> 02:38:14.047
look again. What will help you understand
their access, participation and

02:38:14.048 --> 02:38:19.029
experience? Include their perspective in a
way that works for them. Keep the

02:38:19.030 --> 02:38:23.812
action small, testable and open to
revision. Your Continue and Reconsider

02:38:23.813 --> 02:38:29.192
are useful alongside it: one recognises a
practice worth sustaining, and the other

02:38:29.193 --> 02:38:30.852
keeps your learning open.

02:38:32.360 --> 02:38:37.556
Now plan the next conversation on page
twenty-eight. Bring forward your Session

02:38:37.557 --> 02:38:42.286
Five bridge notes from page twenty-five
and the process change from your

02:38:42.287 --> 02:38:47.150
power-dynamics reflection on page
twenty-three. Choose a real conversation

02:38:47.151 --> 02:38:52.613
within two weeks, with a student's family
or with the student themselves. Write its

02:38:52.614 --> 02:38:57.676
purpose in one sentence. If it is with a
family, plan it with the teacher, or

02:38:57.677 --> 02:39:02.939
record how the teacher will be involved.
You might begin with the warm handover:

02:39:02.940 --> 02:39:08.136
'That matters. Can we bring the teacher
into this together?' Plan what you will

02:39:08.137 --> 02:39:13.266
share, beginning with a strength; what you
will ask specifically; and what you

02:39:13.267 --> 02:39:18.663
will offer. Keep offers within your role.
Anything that changes the student's plan

02:39:18.664 --> 02:39:23.326
needs the teacher involved. Include how
the student's communication and

02:39:23.327 --> 02:39:28.390
preferences will be heard. Write your
opening sentence word for word. Does it

02:39:28.391 --> 02:39:32.987
invite something you don't yet know? Leave
room for the other person's

02:39:32.988 --> 02:39:37.717
knowledge to change your understanding.
Apply your process change to the

02:39:37.718 --> 02:39:42.714
arrangements, and note when, where and how
the conversation will happen. Use

02:39:42.715 --> 02:39:47.577
initials or roles only, and share your
notes only if you wish. Allow eight

02:39:47.578 --> 02:39:50.842
minutes. Pause here to make the plan, then
resume.

02:39:58.880 --> 02:40:03.826
Take a final look at your conversation
plan. Have you prepared to listen, as

02:40:03.827 --> 02:40:08.905
well as to speak? Does the plan leave
something meaningful to decide together?

02:40:08.906 --> 02:40:13.918
Have you made a way for the student's own
perspective to be included? You can

02:40:13.919 --> 02:40:18.865
prepare your opening and your questions
without scripting the other person's

02:40:18.866 --> 02:40:23.351
response. If new knowledge changes the
next step you had in mind, the

02:40:23.352 --> 02:40:28.430
conversation is doing useful work. Keep
the time and follow-up visible in your

02:40:28.431 --> 02:40:33.772
plan. One action in your support role and
one conversation now connect what you've

02:40:33.773 --> 02:40:37.796
learned today to the people and setting
where you will use it.

02:40:39.320 --> 02:40:44.076
This slide is the place for Caitlin's
account of how teacher belief shaped

02:40:44.077 --> 02:40:49.160
their success. That video hasn't been
supplied in this version of the workshop,

02:40:49.161 --> 02:40:53.852
so we'll move directly to the final
reflection. Carry its focus with you:

02:40:53.853 --> 02:40:58.870
teacher belief, student experience, and
the difference our responses can make.

02:41:00.400 --> 02:41:05.643
Return to page five of your Participant
Guide, My starting point. Earlier, you

02:41:05.644 --> 02:41:10.820
wrote about what school is for and what
you were curious about. Read your own

02:41:10.821 --> 02:41:15.587
words back. Now turn to the final
reflection on page twenty-nine. Where

02:41:15.588 --> 02:41:20.491
were you when we began? Where are you now?
What's different in how you're

02:41:20.492 --> 02:41:25.939
thinking about the students you have in
mind? Take a little quiet time with those

02:41:25.940 --> 02:41:30.707
questions. Then write one sentence, just
for yourself: what will you do

02:41:30.708 --> 02:41:36.019
differently in your work with students?
This reflection can stay private. There

02:41:36.020 --> 02:41:41.604
is no need to share it. The live workshop
allows four minutes here. Pause the video

02:41:41.605 --> 02:41:45.213
for the time you need, then resume when
you are ready.

02:41:53.240 --> 02:41:57.575
Keep that sentence with your action card.
It may capture a shift you want to

02:41:57.576 --> 02:42:02.199
remember when you're back in the pace of a
school day. You can return to it after

02:42:02.200 --> 02:42:06.419
you've tried your change and had your
conversation, and notice what you've

02:42:06.420 --> 02:42:07.170
learned then.

02:42:08.680 --> 02:42:13.835
What are you curious to learn more about
next? Look back at your questions on page

02:42:13.836 --> 02:42:18.864
five. Which have been answered, and which
have become more specific? Page thirty

02:42:18.865 --> 02:42:23.448
brings together pathways for continuing
the learning. Webinars and deeper

02:42:23.449 --> 02:42:28.158
sessions can help you explore topics such
as Universal Design for Learning,

02:42:28.159 --> 02:42:33.378
executive functioning, sensory design and
anxiety. The Online Community of Practice

02:42:33.379 --> 02:42:37.962
is intended as a place to keep talking
with other educators. The Positive

02:42:37.963 --> 02:42:42.482
Partnerships website also provides
resources for educators, families and

02:42:42.483 --> 02:42:47.511
students. The particular webinar titles
and dates, and the Community of Practice

02:42:47.512 --> 02:42:52.221
link, are still awaiting confirmation in
these materials. Use the confirmed

02:42:52.222 --> 02:42:56.805
follow-up information from your workshop
organiser when those details are

02:42:56.806 --> 02:43:01.643
available. If photographs of wall work are
shared, check that they contain no

02:43:01.644 --> 02:43:06.608
student details and confirm where your
group can find them. For now, choose the

02:43:06.609 --> 02:43:11.765
pathway that fits your next question. You
can begin with the resources at positive

02:43:11.766 --> 02:43:16.666
partnerships dot com dot au. Today is a
starting point for continued learning,

02:43:16.667 --> 02:43:18.385
reflection and partnership.

02:43:19.920 --> 02:43:24.775
Here are the Positive Partnerships website
and social channels. You can note

02:43:24.776 --> 02:43:29.955
the details, take a screenshot, or return
to this slide later. The slide includes

02:43:29.956 --> 02:43:34.877
Facebook, Instagram, Threads, LinkedIn and
Pinterest, as well as the website.

02:43:34.878 --> 02:43:39.862
Choose the way of staying connected that
works for you. Pause the video if you

02:43:39.863 --> 02:43:42.581
would like longer with the contact
details.

02:43:45.600 --> 02:43:50.493
Before we close, there is time for
feedback. Your reflections can help the

02:43:50.494 --> 02:43:55.320
team understand what was useful, what
needs clarification, and what could

02:43:55.321 --> 02:44:00.415
improve the workshop experience. The
feedback link is on this slide, with the

02:44:00.416 --> 02:44:05.309
code you can scan. If that way of
accessing the form doesn't work for you,

02:44:05.310 --> 02:44:10.606
let your workshop organiser know so you
can discuss an accessible way to provide

02:44:10.607 --> 02:44:15.969
your feedback. The live workshop protects
four minutes for this. If your group is

02:44:15.970 --> 02:44:20.930
using the form, pause the video now and
take that time. Then resume for our

02:44:20.931 --> 02:44:21.869
closing words.

02:44:29.880 --> 02:44:34.599
Thank you for taking the time to reflect
on the workshop. Keep any questions you

02:44:34.600 --> 02:44:39.319
still want to explore alongside your next
steps, so they can inform the learning

02:44:39.320 --> 02:44:40.931
that continues after today.

02:44:42.440 --> 02:44:47.095
Thank you for your attention, questions
and the close knowledge of students you

02:44:47.096 --> 02:44:51.512
brought to these sessions. Take your
action card and conversation plan with

02:44:51.513 --> 02:44:56.347
you, along with the sentence you wrote in
your final reflection. Stay curious, and

02:44:56.348 --> 02:45:00.944
keep students at the centre. Take one
thoughtful next step, include the people

02:45:00.945 --> 02:45:05.659
who hold relevant knowledge, and review
what happens. Student voice, observation

02:45:05.660 --> 02:45:10.256
and partnership can help you refine a
small change into something that makes a

02:45:10.257 --> 02:45:15.031
meaningful difference. Use the resources
and the confirmed follow-up arrangements

02:45:15.032 --> 02:45:19.209
from your workshop team to stay connected.
You don't need to have every

02:45:19.210 --> 02:45:23.985
answer before you begin listening, asking
a better question or changing something

02:45:23.986 --> 02:45:28.462
within your influence. Good luck with your
next steps, and thank you for the

02:45:28.463 --> 02:45:30.132
care you bring to this work.

